(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("cloud",{"cloud/cloud-fundamentals":{"html":"\u003cp>Cloud platforms rent you computing by the second instead of selling you servers. The vocabulary differs between providers, but the ideas are the same. Learn them once and you can read either AWS or Azure documentation without starting over.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Regions, availability zones and why they exist\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>IaaS, PaaS and SaaS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The shared responsibility model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How accounts and subscriptions organize resources\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"regions-and-availability-zones\">Regions and availability zones\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>region\u003c/strong> is a geographic area with several data centers, such as \u003ccode>eu-west-1\u003c/code> (Ireland) in AWS or \u003ccode>westeurope\u003c/code> in Azure. An \u003cstrong>availability zone (AZ)\u003c/strong> is one or more separate data centers within a region, with independent power and networking, connected by fast links.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Concept\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Design rule\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Region\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Latency, data residency, disaster recovery\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Choose near users and within legal requirements\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Availability zone\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Survive a data center failure\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Spread each important service across at least two zones\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A service running in a single zone is one power failure away from an outage. Running across zones is the basic form of high availability. Surviving the loss of a whole region is a larger, more expensive design that you adopt only when the business needs it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"service-models\">Service models\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Model\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>You manage\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Provider manages\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Examples\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>IaaS\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Operating system, runtime, application\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hardware, virtualization\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>AWS EC2, Azure Virtual Machines\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>PaaS\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Application and data\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Everything below, including patching\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>AWS Elastic Beanstalk, Azure App Service, managed databases\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>SaaS\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Your usage and data\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The whole application\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Email, ticketing, CRM products\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Moving toward PaaS reduces operational work and risk, at the cost of some control. A good default is the most managed service that meets your requirements.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shared-responsibility\">Shared responsibility\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The provider secures the cloud itself: buildings, hardware, hypervisors, the global network. \u003cstrong>You secure what you put in it\u003c/strong>: identities and permissions, data, network rules, operating system patches on virtual machines, and configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-why-it-matters\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Why it matters\u003c/div>\u003cp>Most publicly reported cloud breaches involve customer configuration, such as a storage bucket opened to the internet or an access key committed to a shared location, not a failure of the provider&#39;s data centers.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"organizing-accounts\">Organizing accounts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>AWS\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Azure\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Top-level grouping\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Organization\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Tenant (Microsoft Entra ID) and management groups\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Billing and isolation boundary\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Account\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Subscription\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Logical folder inside\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Tags, resource groups (optional)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Resource group (required)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Policy at scale\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Service control policies\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure Policy\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>separate accounts or subscriptions\u003c/strong> for production and non-production, and often per team or workload. A boundary at this level limits the blast radius of mistakes and of compromised credentials far better than any tag could.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost-basics\">Cost basics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cloud billing rewards switching things off. The main levers:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Right-size\u003c/strong>: match instance sizes to measured usage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Commit\u003c/strong>: reserved capacity or savings plans for steady workloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Spot or low-priority capacity\u003c/strong> for interruptible jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Delete\u003c/strong> unattached disks, old snapshots and idle load balancers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Tag\u003c/strong> resources with owner and environment, so you can tell whose bill is whose\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Set a \u003cstrong>budget alert\u003c/strong> on every account on day one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-an-unexpected-bill\">Incident: an unexpected bill\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The monthly invoice is four times higher than usual, and nobody launched anything new.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Start with where the cost is, not who to blame. In AWS use Cost Explorer grouped by service and by region; in Azure use Cost Analysis grouped by resource group and meter.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws ce get-cost-and-usage \\\n    --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-10-24 \\\n    --granularity DAILY --metrics UnblendedCost \\\n    --group-by Type=DIMENSION,Key=SERVICE\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ce get-cost-and-usage \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-10-24 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --granularity DAILY --metrics UnblendedCost \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --group-by Type=DIMENSION,Key=SERVICE\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output shows a large increase in data transfer and compute in an unfamiliar region. Unfamiliar region plus compute is a classic sign of leaked credentials being used to mine cryptocurrency. Treat it as a security incident: disable the exposed keys immediately, review activity in CloudTrail (or the Azure Activity Log), terminate unknown resources, and rotate every credential that could have been exposed. Afterward, add controls that would have caught it sooner: budget alerts at low thresholds, a policy denying use of regions you do not operate in, and short-lived credentials instead of long-lived keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running everything in one account or subscription\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Placing production in a single availability zone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No budget alert and no tagging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming the provider secures your configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"regions-and-availability-zones","text":"Regions and availability zones"},{"id":"service-models","text":"Service models"},{"id":"shared-responsibility","text":"Shared responsibility"},{"id":"organizing-accounts","text":"Organizing accounts"},{"id":"cost-basics","text":"Cost basics"},{"id":"incident-an-unexpected-bill","text":"Incident: an unexpected bill"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS shared responsibility model","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/shared-responsibility-model/"},{"t":"AWS Well-Architected Framework","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/welcome.html"},{"t":"Azure Well-Architected Framework","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/"}],"text":"Cloud platforms rent you computing by the second instead of selling you servers. The vocabulary differs between providers, but the ideas are the same. Learn them once and you can read either AWS or Azure documentation without starting over. What you will learn Regions, availability zones and why they exist IaaS, PaaS and SaaS The shared responsibility model How accounts and subscriptions organize resources Regions and availability zones A region is a geographic area with several data centers, such as eu-west-1 (Ireland) in AWS or westeurope in Azure. An availability zone (AZ) is one or more separate data centers within a region, with independent power and networking, connected by fast links. Concept Purpose Design rule Region Latency, data residency, disaster recovery Choose near users and within legal requirements Availability zone Survive a data center failure Spread each important service across at least two zones A service running in a single zone is one power failure away from an outage. Running across zones is the basic form of high availability. Surviving the loss of a whole region is a larger, more expensive design that you adopt only when the business needs it. Service models Model You manage Provider manages Examples IaaS Operating system, runtime, application Hardware, virtualization AWS EC2, Azure Virtual Machines PaaS Application and data Everything below, including patching AWS Elastic Beanstalk, Azure App Service, managed databases SaaS Your usage and data The whole application Email, ticketing, CRM products Moving toward PaaS reduces operational work and risk, at the cost of some control. A good default is the most managed service that meets your requirements. Shared responsibility The provider secures the cloud itself: buildings, hardware, hypervisors, the global network. You secure what you put in it : identities and permissions, data, network rules, operating system patches on virtual machines, and configuration. Why it matters Most publicly reported cloud breaches involve customer configuration, such as a storage bucket opened to the internet or an access key committed to a shared location, not a failure of the provider&#39;s data centers. Organizing accounts AWS Azure Top-level grouping Organization Tenant (Microsoft Entra ID) and management groups Billing and isolation boundary Account Subscription Logical folder inside Tags, resource groups (optional) Resource group (required) Policy at scale Service control policies Azure Policy Use separate accounts or subscriptions for production and non-production, and often per team or workload. A boundary at this level limits the blast radius of mistakes and of compromised credentials far better than any tag could. Cost basics Cloud billing rewards switching things off. The main levers: Right-size : match instance sizes to measured usage Commit : reserved capacity or savings plans for steady workloads Spot or low-priority capacity for interruptible jobs Delete unattached disks, old snapshots and idle load balancers Tag resources with owner and environment, so you can tell whose bill is whose Set a budget alert on every account on day one. Incident: an unexpected bill Incident The monthly invoice is four times higher than usual, and nobody launched anything new. Start with where the cost is, not who to blame. In AWS use Cost Explorer grouped by service and by region; in Azure use Cost Analysis grouped by resource group and meter. $ aws ce get-cost-and-usage \\ --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-10-24 \\ --granularity DAILY --metrics UnblendedCost \\ --group-by Type=DIMENSION,Key=SERVICE The output shows a large increase in data transfer and compute in an unfamiliar region. Unfamiliar region plus compute is a classic sign of leaked credentials being used to mine cryptocurrency. Treat it as a security incident: disable the exposed keys immediately, review activity in CloudTrail (or the Azure Activity Log), terminate unknown resources, and rotate every credential tha"},"cloud/identity-and-access":{"html":"\u003cp>Identity and access management is the foundation of cloud security. Networks can be misconfigured and software can have bugs, but if every identity has exactly the access it needs and no more, the damage from either is limited.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Users, groups, roles and policies in AWS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Entra ID, RBAC and managed identities in Azure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why roles and short-lived credentials beat access keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to troubleshoot an access denied error\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"aws-iam\">AWS IAM\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Concept\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>User\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A long-lived identity for a person or program. Avoid for programs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Group\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A set of users sharing permissions\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Role\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An identity that is \u003cstrong>assumed\u003c/strong> temporarily. Used by services, applications and federated people\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Policy\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A JSON document that allows or denies actions on resources\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A policy states which \u003cstrong>actions\u003c/strong> are allowed on which \u003cstrong>resources\u003c/strong>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n  &quot;Statement&quot;: [\n    {\n      &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n      &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;, &quot;s3:PutObject&quot;],\n      &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::acme-uploads/shop/*&quot;\n    }\n  ]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Version&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2012-10-17&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Statement&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Effect&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Allow&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Action&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;s3:PutObject&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Resource&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;arn:aws:s3:::acme-uploads/shop/*&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Evaluation follows a clear rule: everything is \u003cstrong>denied by default\u003c/strong>; an explicit \u003ccode>Allow\u003c/code> is needed; an explicit \u003ccode>Deny\u003c/code> always wins. Permission boundaries, service control policies and session policies can further restrict what an identity can do, even when its own policy allows it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An EC2 instance, a Lambda function or a Kubernetes service account gets permissions by being given a \u003cstrong>role\u003c/strong>. The credentials it receives are temporary and rotate automatically, so there is no key to leak.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"azure-access-control\">Azure access control\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Azure splits identity from authorization:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Concept\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Microsoft Entra ID\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The identity directory for users, groups and applications\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Azure RBAC\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Role assignments at a scope: management group, subscription, resource group or resource\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Managed identity\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An identity for an Azure resource, with no secret to manage\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Role assignment\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Who (principal) + which role + at what scope\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Built-in roles include Reader, Contributor and Owner, and many specific ones. Assign the narrowest role at the narrowest useful scope.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"az role assignment create \\\n    --assignee-object-id &quot;$PRINCIPAL_ID&quot; \\\n    --assignee-principal-type ServicePrincipal \\\n    --role &quot;Storage Blob Data Contributor&quot; \\\n    --scope /subscriptions/$SUB/resourceGroups/shop-prod/providers/Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/acmeuploads\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az role assignment create \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --assignee-object-id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$PRINCIPAL_ID\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --assignee-principal-type ServicePrincipal \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --role \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Storage Blob Data Contributor&quot;\u003c/span> \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --scope /subscriptions/\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$SUB\u003c/span>/resourceGroups/shop-prod/providers/Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/acmeuploads\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"principles-that-apply-everywhere\">Principles that apply everywhere\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Least privilege.\u003c/strong> Start with nothing and add what is needed; do not start with admin and remove.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Prefer roles and managed identities\u003c/strong> over access keys and passwords.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Require multi-factor authentication\u003c/strong> for every human, and protect the root or global administrator account with hardware keys and no daily use.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Use groups\u003c/strong> for people, so access follows job role rather than individuals.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Review regularly.\u003c/strong> Remove unused identities and permissions. Both clouds report last-used information.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Log everything\u003c/strong>: CloudTrail in AWS, Activity Log and Entra sign-in logs in Azure.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>An access key that appears in a shared location, a build log or an image must be treated as compromised. Deactivate it immediately, then investigate what it did, then replace it with a role.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verifying-identity-and-permissions\">Verifying identity and permissions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws sts get-caller-identity\naz account show --query &quot;{subscription:name, user:user.name}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws sts get-caller-identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    &quot;UserId&quot;: &quot;AROAEXAMPLE:session-1&quot;,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    &quot;Account&quot;: &quot;123456789012&quot;,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    &quot;Arn&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/shop-api-role/session-1&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az account show --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{subscription:name, user:user.name}&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Always check \u003cstrong>who you are\u003c/strong> before debugging \u003cstrong>what you are allowed to do\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-accessdenied-when-the-application-reads-a-bucket\">Incident: AccessDenied when the application reads a bucket\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The API returns errors. The log says \u003ccode>AccessDenied: User: arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/shop-api-role/i-0abc is not authorized to perform: s3:GetObject on resource: arn:aws:s3:::acme-uploads/shop/logo.png\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The error message names the identity, the action and the resource, which is everything you need. Work through the layers:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Is the identity what you expect? Here the role is \u003ccode>shop-api-role\u003c/code>. Confirm the application is really using it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Does an identity policy allow the action? The earlier policy allows \u003ccode>GetObject\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>shop/*\u003c/code>, which matches this object.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Does the bucket have a resource policy with a \u003ccode>Deny\u003c/code>, or an encryption setting requiring access to a KMS key?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do organization-level controls (service control policies or permission boundaries) block it?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam simulate-principal-policy \\\n    --policy-source-arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/shop-api-role \\\n    --action-names s3:GetObject \\\n    --resource-arns arn:aws:s3:::acme-uploads/shop/logo.png\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam simulate-principal-policy \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --policy-source-arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/shop-api-role \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --action-names s3:GetObject \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --resource-arns arn:aws:s3:::acme-uploads/shop/logo.png\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The simulation reports an implicit deny: the policy on the role lists the key prefix \u003ccode>shop/\u003c/code> but the real object is stored at \u003ccode>Shop/logo.png\u003c/code>, and S3 key names are case-sensitive. Correct the resource path in the policy or the application, and the request succeeds. If the cause had been KMS, the error would have mentioned the key. Reading the denied action and resource carefully saves hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"predict-the-access\">Predict the access\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A policy allows s3:GetObject on a bucket, and another policy has an explicit Deny for the same action. What is the result?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Access is denied.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An explicit deny always wins over any allow. Use it for guardrails.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A server needs to read one bucket. Which is safer: an access key in a config file, or a role attached to the instance?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The role attached to the instance.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A role gives short-lived credentials that rotate automatically and are not stored on disk.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the root account or a global administrator for daily work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Wildcard actions and resources (\u003ccode>&quot;Action&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\u003c/code>) in policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long-lived access keys stored in applications\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting access to individuals instead of groups\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"aws-iam","text":"AWS IAM"},{"id":"azure-access-control","text":"Azure access control"},{"id":"principles-that-apply-everywhere","text":"Principles that apply everywhere"},{"id":"verifying-identity-and-permissions","text":"Verifying identity and permissions"},{"id":"incident-accessdenied-when-the-application-reads-a-bucket","text":"Incident: AccessDenied when the application reads a bucket"},{"id":"predict-the-access","text":"Predict the access"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS IAM user guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/introduction.html"},{"t":"AWS IAM: security best practices","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html"},{"t":"Azure RBAC overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview"}],"text":"Identity and access management is the foundation of cloud security. Networks can be misconfigured and software can have bugs, but if every identity has exactly the access it needs and no more, the damage from either is limited. What you will learn Users, groups, roles and policies in AWS Entra ID, RBAC and managed identities in Azure Why roles and short-lived credentials beat access keys How to troubleshoot an access denied error AWS IAM Concept Meaning User A long-lived identity for a person or program. Avoid for programs Group A set of users sharing permissions Role An identity that is assumed temporarily. Used by services, applications and federated people Policy A JSON document that allows or denies actions on resources A policy states which actions are allowed on which resources : { \"Version\" : \"2012-10-17\" , \"Statement\" : [ { \"Effect\" : \"Allow\" , \"Action\" : [ \"s3:GetObject\" , \"s3:PutObject\" ] , \"Resource\" : \"arn:aws:s3:::acme-uploads/shop/*\" } ] } Evaluation follows a clear rule: everything is denied by default ; an explicit Allow is needed; an explicit Deny always wins. Permission boundaries, service control policies and session policies can further restrict what an identity can do, even when its own policy allows it. An EC2 instance, a Lambda function or a Kubernetes service account gets permissions by being given a role . The credentials it receives are temporary and rotate automatically, so there is no key to leak. Azure access control Azure splits identity from authorization: Concept Meaning Microsoft Entra ID The identity directory for users, groups and applications Azure RBAC Role assignments at a scope: management group, subscription, resource group or resource Managed identity An identity for an Azure resource, with no secret to manage Role assignment Who (principal) + which role + at what scope Built-in roles include Reader, Contributor and Owner, and many specific ones. Assign the narrowest role at the narrowest useful scope. $ az role assignment create \\ --assignee-object-id \" $PRINCIPAL_ID \" \\ --assignee-principal-type ServicePrincipal \\ --role \"Storage Blob Data Contributor\" \\ --scope /subscriptions/ $SUB /resourceGroups/shop-prod/providers/Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/acmeuploads Principles that apply everywhere Least privilege. Start with nothing and add what is needed; do not start with admin and remove. Prefer roles and managed identities over access keys and passwords. Require multi-factor authentication for every human, and protect the root or global administrator account with hardware keys and no daily use. Use groups for people, so access follows job role rather than individuals. Review regularly. Remove unused identities and permissions. Both clouds report last-used information. Log everything : CloudTrail in AWS, Activity Log and Entra sign-in logs in Azure. Warning An access key that appears in a shared location, a build log or an image must be treated as compromised. Deactivate it immediately, then investigate what it did, then replace it with a role. Verifying identity and permissions $ aws sts get-caller-identity { \"UserId\": \"AROAEXAMPLE:session-1\", \"Account\": \"123456789012\", \"Arn\": \"arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/shop-api-role/session-1\" } $ az account show --query \"{subscription:name, user:user.name}\" Always check who you are before debugging what you are allowed to do . Incident: AccessDenied when the application reads a bucket Incident The API returns errors. The log says AccessDenied: User: arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/shop-api-role/i-0abc is not authorized to perform: s3:GetObject on resource: arn:aws:s3:::acme-uploads/shop/logo.png . The error message names the identity, the action and the resource, which is everything you need. Work through the layers: Is the identity what you expect? Here the role is shop-api-role . Confirm the application is really using it. Does an identity policy allow the action? The earlier policy allows GetObject on shop/* , which matches "},"cloud/virtual-networks":{"html":"\u003cp>Every cloud workload lives in a virtual network that you design. The layout decides what is reachable from the internet, what can talk to what internally, and how much a single mistake can expose. It builds directly on the addressing, routing and firewall ideas from the networking module.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>VPC and VNet structure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Public and private subnets, route tables, gateways\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Security groups, NACLs and network security groups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A reachability checklist\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-building-blocks\">The building blocks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Idea\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>AWS\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Azure\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>The network\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VPC\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Virtual network (VNet)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>A slice of it\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Subnet (lives in one AZ)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Subnet (spans the zones of the region)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Routing\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Route table per subnet\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Route table (UDRs) per subnet\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Internet access in\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Internet gateway\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Public IP on the resource\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Outbound only from private subnets\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>NAT gateway\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>NAT gateway\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Connect networks\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VPC peering, Transit Gateway\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VNet peering, Virtual WAN\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Private access to cloud services\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VPC endpoints (PrivateLink)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Private endpoints\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Instance-level firewall\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Security group (stateful)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Network security group on NIC or subnet (stateful)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Subnet-level firewall\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Network ACL (stateless)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>NSG on a subnet\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-standard-layout\">A standard layout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"VPC 10.20.0.0/16\n  Public subnets   (10.20.0.0/24, 10.20.1.0/24)   one per AZ\n     load balancers, NAT gateways\n  Private app subnets (10.20.10.0/24, 10.20.11.0/24)\n     application servers or Kubernetes nodes\n  Private data subnets (10.20.20.0/24, 10.20.21.0/24)\n     databases, no route to the internet\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">VPC 10.20.0.0/16\n  Public subnets   (10.20.0.0/24, 10.20.1.0/24)   one per AZ\n     load balancers, NAT gateways\n  Private app subnets (10.20.10.0/24, 10.20.11.0/24)\n     application servers or Kubernetes nodes\n  Private data subnets (10.20.20.0/24, 10.20.21.0/24)\n     databases, no route to the internet\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A subnet is &quot;public&quot; because its route table sends \u003ccode>0.0.0.0/0\u003c/code> to an internet gateway \u003cstrong>and\u003c/strong> its resources have public addresses. A &quot;private&quot; subnet has no such route; if it must reach the internet for updates it sends \u003ccode>0.0.0.0/0\u003c/code> to a NAT gateway in a public subnet. Databases usually need no internet route at all.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Plan address ranges so that networks you will later connect (other VPCs, on-premises sites) \u003cstrong>do not overlap\u003c/strong>. Overlapping ranges cannot be peered, and renumbering later is painful.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"security-rules\">Security rules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Security groups and NSGs are stateful: if a connection is allowed in, the return traffic is allowed automatically. Reference other groups instead of IP ranges where you can.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resource &quot;aws_security_group_rule&quot; &quot;db_from_app&quot; {\n  type                     = &quot;ingress&quot;\n  security_group_id        = aws_security_group.db.id\n  source_security_group_id = aws_security_group.app.id\n  from_port                = 5432\n  to_port                  = 5432\n  protocol                 = &quot;tcp&quot;\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_security_group_rule&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db_from_app&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span>                     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ingress&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  security_group_id\u003c/span>        = aws_security_group.db.id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  source_security_group_id\u003c/span> = aws_security_group.app.id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  from_port\u003c/span>                = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5432\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  to_port\u003c/span>                  = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5432\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  protocol\u003c/span>                 = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tcp&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This says &quot;the database accepts PostgreSQL traffic from anything in the app group&quot;, which stays correct as application servers come and go.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Do not open SSH (22) or RDP (3389) to \u003ccode>0.0.0.0/0\u003c/code>. Use a bastion with strict source ranges, or better, a session service that needs no inbound port (AWS Systems Manager Session Manager, Azure Bastion).\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reachability-checklist\">Reachability checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When A cannot reach B, check in this order:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Name resolution\u003c/strong>: does the name resolve to the address you expect?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Route\u003c/strong>: does A&#39;s subnet route table have a path to B&#39;s range?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Source firewall\u003c/strong>: does A&#39;s outbound rule allow it? (Defaults usually do.)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Destination firewall\u003c/strong>: does B&#39;s security group or NSG allow A on that port?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Subnet filters\u003c/strong>: do network ACLs or subnet NSGs allow both directions (stateless ACLs need return rules)?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Listener\u003c/strong>: is something actually listening on that port on B?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Cloud tools can analyze the path for you: AWS Reachability Analyzer and Azure Network Watcher (connection troubleshoot and IP flow verify).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-new-service-cannot-reach-its-database\">Incident: the new service cannot reach its database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new application server in subnet \u003ccode>10.20.10.0/24\u003c/code> times out connecting to PostgreSQL at \u003ccode>10.20.20.15:5432\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>A timeout (not &quot;connection refused&quot;) means packets are being dropped before reaching a listener, which points at a route or firewall. Test from the app server:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nc -zv -w 3 10.20.20.15 5432\naws ec2 describe-security-groups --group-ids sg-0db123 \\\n    --query 'SecurityGroups[0].IpPermissions[].{port:FromPort,from:UserIdGroupPairs[].GroupId}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>nc -zv -w 3 10.20.20.15 5432\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nc: connect to 10.20.20.15 port 5432 (tcp) failed: Connection timed out\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ec2 describe-security-groups --group-ids sg-0db123 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;SecurityGroups[0].IpPermissions[].{port:FromPort,from:UserIdGroupPairs[].GroupId}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    { &quot;port&quot;: 5432, &quot;from&quot;: [&quot;sg-0app111&quot;] }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The database group allows port 5432 only from \u003ccode>sg-0app111\u003c/code>, but the new server was launched with a different group, \u003ccode>sg-0app222\u003c/code>. Attach the correct group (or add a rule referencing the new one), retest with \u003ccode>nc\u003c/code>, and confirm the application connects. Because the rule referenced a group rather than a fixed IP, fixing group membership was enough. A &quot;refused&quot; error instead would have meant traffic arrived but nothing listened, which sends you to the database process rather than the network.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"net&gt;pub;pub&gt;app;app&gt;db;app&gt;nat\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A three tier network in the cloud\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"net\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Internet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pub\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Public subnet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Private app subnet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"db\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Private data subnet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"nat\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">NAT gateway\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Internet to Public subnet; 2, Public subnet to Private app subnet; 3, Private app subnet to Private data subnet; 4, Private app subnet to NAT gateway.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"net\">\u003cdt>Internet \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Users reach the public load balancer.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Open rules beyond 80 and 443 expose more.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pub\">\u003cdt>Public subnet \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds load balancers and NAT.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A server here with a public IP is directly exposed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Private app subnet \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Application servers with no public IP.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing NAT means they cannot download updates.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"db\">\u003cdt>Private data subnet \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Databases that accept traffic only from the app tier.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A broad rule exposes data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"nat\">\u003cdt>NAT gateway \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Allows outbound traffic from private subnets.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it fails, patches and external calls stop.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Overlapping address ranges between networks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Placing databases in public subnets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Opening administrative ports to the whole internet\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that network ACLs are stateless\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Single NAT gateway or single zone for something that must stay up\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-building-blocks","text":"The building blocks"},{"id":"a-standard-layout","text":"A standard layout"},{"id":"security-rules","text":"Security rules"},{"id":"reachability-checklist","text":"Reachability checklist"},{"id":"incident-the-new-service-cannot-reach-its-database","text":"Incident: the new service cannot reach its database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Amazon VPC user guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/what-is-amazon-vpc.html"},{"t":"Azure Virtual Network","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-networks-overview"},{"t":"AWS: VPC route tables","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/VPC_Route_Tables.html"}],"text":"Every cloud workload lives in a virtual network that you design. The layout decides what is reachable from the internet, what can talk to what internally, and how much a single mistake can expose. It builds directly on the addressing, routing and firewall ideas from the networking module. What you will learn VPC and VNet structure Public and private subnets, route tables, gateways Security groups, NACLs and network security groups A reachability checklist The building blocks Idea AWS Azure The network VPC Virtual network (VNet) A slice of it Subnet (lives in one AZ) Subnet (spans the zones of the region) Routing Route table per subnet Route table (UDRs) per subnet Internet access in Internet gateway Public IP on the resource Outbound only from private subnets NAT gateway NAT gateway Connect networks VPC peering, Transit Gateway VNet peering, Virtual WAN Private access to cloud services VPC endpoints (PrivateLink) Private endpoints Instance-level firewall Security group (stateful) Network security group on NIC or subnet (stateful) Subnet-level firewall Network ACL (stateless) NSG on a subnet A standard layout VPC 10.20.0.0/16 Public subnets (10.20.0.0/24, 10.20.1.0/24) one per AZ load balancers, NAT gateways Private app subnets (10.20.10.0/24, 10.20.11.0/24) application servers or Kubernetes nodes Private data subnets (10.20.20.0/24, 10.20.21.0/24) databases, no route to the internet A subnet is \"public\" because its route table sends 0.0.0.0/0 to an internet gateway and its resources have public addresses. A \"private\" subnet has no such route; if it must reach the internet for updates it sends 0.0.0.0/0 to a NAT gateway in a public subnet. Databases usually need no internet route at all. Plan address ranges so that networks you will later connect (other VPCs, on-premises sites) do not overlap . Overlapping ranges cannot be peered, and renumbering later is painful. Security rules Security groups and NSGs are stateful: if a connection is allowed in, the return traffic is allowed automatically. Reference other groups instead of IP ranges where you can. resource \"aws_security_group_rule\" \"db_from_app\" { type = \"ingress\" security_group_id = aws_security_group.db.id source_security_group_id = aws_security_group.app.id from_port = 5432 to_port = 5432 protocol = \"tcp\" } This says \"the database accepts PostgreSQL traffic from anything in the app group\", which stays correct as application servers come and go. Warning Do not open SSH (22) or RDP (3389) to 0.0.0.0/0 . Use a bastion with strict source ranges, or better, a session service that needs no inbound port (AWS Systems Manager Session Manager, Azure Bastion). Reachability checklist When A cannot reach B, check in this order: Name resolution : does the name resolve to the address you expect? Route : does A&#39;s subnet route table have a path to B&#39;s range? Source firewall : does A&#39;s outbound rule allow it? (Defaults usually do.) Destination firewall : does B&#39;s security group or NSG allow A on that port? Subnet filters : do network ACLs or subnet NSGs allow both directions (stateless ACLs need return rules)? Listener : is something actually listening on that port on B? Cloud tools can analyze the path for you: AWS Reachability Analyzer and Azure Network Watcher (connection troubleshoot and IP flow verify). Incident: the new service cannot reach its database Incident A new application server in subnet 10.20.10.0/24 times out connecting to PostgreSQL at 10.20.20.15:5432 . A timeout (not \"connection refused\") means packets are being dropped before reaching a listener, which points at a route or firewall. Test from the app server: $ nc -zv -w 3 10.20.20.15 5432 nc: connect to 10.20.20.15 port 5432 (tcp) failed: Connection timed out $ aws ec2 describe-security-groups --group-ids sg-0db123 \\ --query &#x27;SecurityGroups[0].IpPermissions[].{port:FromPort,from:UserIdGroupPairs[].GroupId}&#x27; [ { \"port\": 5432, \"from\": [\"sg-0app111\"] } ] The database group allows port 5432 only fr"},"cloud/compute-and-storage":{"html":"\u003cp>Two decisions come up in every design: where the code runs, and where the data lives. Each has several options on both clouds, and choosing well is mostly about matching the workload to the level of management you want to carry.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The compute options and when each fits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Autoscaling and load balancing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Object, block and file storage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protecting data from loss and exposure\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compute-options\">Compute options\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>AWS\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Azure\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Choose when\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Virtual machines\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>EC2\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Virtual Machines\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>You need full OS control or run legacy software\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Managed containers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>ECS, Fargate\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Container Apps, Container Instances\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>You want containers without running a cluster\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Managed Kubernetes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>EKS\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>AKS\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>You need Kubernetes features and a portable platform\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Functions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Lambda\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure Functions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Short, event-driven work with spiky traffic\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Managed web platform\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Elastic Beanstalk, App Runner\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>App Service\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A web app with minimal operations\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A rule of thumb: pick the \u003cstrong>most managed\u003c/strong> option that still fits. A function or managed container has less to patch than a virtual machine. Reach for virtual machines when you must, and for Kubernetes when you have enough services and enough people to justify its cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scaling-and-load-balancing\">Scaling and load balancing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Put instances behind a \u003cstrong>load balancer\u003c/strong> across at least two zones, and let an \u003cstrong>autoscaling group\u003c/strong> (AWS) or \u003cstrong>scale set\u003c/strong> (Azure) add or remove instances based on a metric such as CPU or request count. Health checks remove bad instances automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws autoscaling describe-auto-scaling-groups \\\n    --query 'AutoScalingGroups[].{name:AutoScalingGroupName,min:MinSize,max:MaxSize,desired:DesiredCapacity}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws autoscaling describe-auto-scaling-groups \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;AutoScalingGroups[].{name:AutoScalingGroupName,min:MinSize,max:MaxSize,desired:DesiredCapacity}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Instances should be \u003cstrong>disposable\u003c/strong>: built from an image or startup script, holding no unique data, and replaceable at any time. If a server is precious, it is a risk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"storage-types\">Storage types\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Type\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>AWS\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Azure\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Use for\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Object\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>S3\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Blob Storage\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Files, backups, static websites, logs, data lakes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Block\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>EBS\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Managed Disks\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A single VM&#39;s disk, databases on VMs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>File\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>EFS, FSx\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure Files\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Shared file systems mounted by many machines\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Object storage is the workhorse: practically unlimited, durable, cheap and accessed over HTTPS. It is not a file system and is not for frequent small edits to one file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Storage \u003cstrong>classes\u003c/strong> trade price for access speed. Frequently read data belongs in the standard tier; old logs belong in an infrequent-access or archive tier, moved by a \u003cstrong>lifecycle rule\u003c/strong> automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resource &quot;aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration&quot; &quot;logs&quot; {\n  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.logs.id\n\n  rule {\n    id     = &quot;archive-then-expire&quot;\n    status = &quot;Enabled&quot;\n\n    filter {}\n\n    transition {\n      days          = 30\n      storage_class = &quot;STANDARD_IA&quot;\n    }\n\n    expiration {\n      days = 365\n    }\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;logs&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  bucket\u003c/span> = aws_s3_bucket.logs.id\n\n  rule {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    id\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;archive-then-expire&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    status\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Enabled&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n    filter {}\n\n    transition {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      days\u003c/span>          = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      storage_class\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;STANDARD_IA&quot;\u003c/span>\n    }\n\n    expiration {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      days\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">365\u003c/span>\n    }\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protecting-data\">Protecting data\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Block public access\u003c/strong> at the account level for object storage, and allow exceptions deliberately.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Encrypt at rest\u003c/strong> with provider-managed or customer-managed keys. Encrypt in transit with TLS.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Turn on versioning\u003c/strong>, so an overwritten or deleted object can be recovered.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Back up\u003c/strong> to a separate account or subscription and \u003cstrong>test restores\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Replicate\u003c/strong> across zones (default for most managed storage) and, where required, across regions.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Snapshot\u003c/strong> disks before risky changes.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Replication copies mistakes as faithfully as it copies data. If someone deletes a file, replication deletes it everywhere. Versioning and backups are what bring it back.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-bucket-is-public\">Incident: a bucket is public\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A security scanner reports that the bucket \u003ccode>acme-reports\u003c/code> allows public reads. It contains customer exports.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Act in this order: close the exposure, then investigate, then fix the cause.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3api put-public-access-block --bucket acme-reports \\\n    --public-access-block-configuration \\\n    BlockPublicAcls=true,IgnorePublicAcls=true,BlockPublicPolicy=true,RestrictPublicBuckets=true\naws s3api get-bucket-policy --bucket acme-reports\naws s3api get-bucket-acl --bucket acme-reports\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api put-public-access-block --bucket acme-reports \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --public-access-block-configuration \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    BlockPublicAcls=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>,IgnorePublicAcls=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>,BlockPublicPolicy=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>,RestrictPublicBuckets=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-bucket-policy --bucket acme-reports\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-bucket-acl --bucket acme-reports\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Blocking public access takes effect immediately and does not delete data. Then find out how long it was open and what was read: check S3 server access logs or CloudTrail data events, if they were enabled. If you had no logging, you cannot prove nothing was accessed and may need to treat the data as disclosed, which can carry legal notification duties. Find the root cause (a policy with \u003ccode>&quot;Principal&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\u003c/code>, a legacy ACL, a deployment that ignored the account-level block) and add controls: account-wide public access block, a policy check in the pipeline, and alerting on any change to bucket exposure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating a virtual machine as a pet that cannot be rebuilt\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No versioning or tested restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Confusing replication with backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving old snapshots and unattached disks to accumulate cost\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"compute-options","text":"Compute options"},{"id":"scaling-and-load-balancing","text":"Scaling and load balancing"},{"id":"storage-types","text":"Storage types"},{"id":"protecting-data","text":"Protecting data"},{"id":"incident-a-bucket-is-public","text":"Incident: a bucket is public"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Amazon EC2 documentation","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ec2/"},{"t":"Azure Virtual Machines","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/"},{"t":"Azure Storage introduction","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-introduction"}],"text":"Two decisions come up in every design: where the code runs, and where the data lives. Each has several options on both clouds, and choosing well is mostly about matching the workload to the level of management you want to carry. What you will learn The compute options and when each fits Autoscaling and load balancing Object, block and file storage Protecting data from loss and exposure Compute options Option AWS Azure Choose when Virtual machines EC2 Virtual Machines You need full OS control or run legacy software Managed containers ECS, Fargate Container Apps, Container Instances You want containers without running a cluster Managed Kubernetes EKS AKS You need Kubernetes features and a portable platform Functions Lambda Azure Functions Short, event-driven work with spiky traffic Managed web platform Elastic Beanstalk, App Runner App Service A web app with minimal operations A rule of thumb: pick the most managed option that still fits. A function or managed container has less to patch than a virtual machine. Reach for virtual machines when you must, and for Kubernetes when you have enough services and enough people to justify its cost. Scaling and load balancing Put instances behind a load balancer across at least two zones, and let an autoscaling group (AWS) or scale set (Azure) add or remove instances based on a metric such as CPU or request count. Health checks remove bad instances automatically. $ aws autoscaling describe-auto-scaling-groups \\ --query &#x27;AutoScalingGroups[].{name:AutoScalingGroupName,min:MinSize,max:MaxSize,desired:DesiredCapacity}&#x27; Instances should be disposable : built from an image or startup script, holding no unique data, and replaceable at any time. If a server is precious, it is a risk. Storage types Type AWS Azure Use for Object S3 Blob Storage Files, backups, static websites, logs, data lakes Block EBS Managed Disks A single VM&#39;s disk, databases on VMs File EFS, FSx Azure Files Shared file systems mounted by many machines Object storage is the workhorse: practically unlimited, durable, cheap and accessed over HTTPS. It is not a file system and is not for frequent small edits to one file. Storage classes trade price for access speed. Frequently read data belongs in the standard tier; old logs belong in an infrequent-access or archive tier, moved by a lifecycle rule automatically. resource \"aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration\" \"logs\" { bucket = aws_s3_bucket.logs.id rule { id = \"archive-then-expire\" status = \"Enabled\" filter {} transition { days = 30 storage_class = \"STANDARD_IA\" } expiration { days = 365 } } } Protecting data Block public access at the account level for object storage, and allow exceptions deliberately. Encrypt at rest with provider-managed or customer-managed keys. Encrypt in transit with TLS. Turn on versioning , so an overwritten or deleted object can be recovered. Back up to a separate account or subscription and test restores . Replicate across zones (default for most managed storage) and, where required, across regions. Snapshot disks before risky changes. Warning Replication copies mistakes as faithfully as it copies data. If someone deletes a file, replication deletes it everywhere. Versioning and backups are what bring it back. Incident: a bucket is public Incident A security scanner reports that the bucket acme-reports allows public reads. It contains customer exports. Act in this order: close the exposure, then investigate, then fix the cause. $ aws s3api put-public-access-block --bucket acme-reports \\ --public-access-block-configuration \\ BlockPublicAcls= true ,IgnorePublicAcls= true ,BlockPublicPolicy= true ,RestrictPublicBuckets= true $ aws s3api get-bucket-policy --bucket acme-reports $ aws s3api get-bucket-acl --bucket acme-reports Blocking public access takes effect immediately and does not delete data. Then find out how long it was open and what was read: check S3 server access logs or CloudTrail data events, if they were enabled. If you had no logg"},"cloud/dns-and-cdn":{"html":"\u003cp>Users reach your application through a name, and often through a content delivery network before they ever touch your servers. Getting DNS, caching and certificates right makes a site faster and far more resilient, while getting them wrong causes outages that are hard to see from inside the cloud.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Managed DNS: hosted zones, record types and alias records\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Routing policies for failover and latency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What a CDN does and how caching is controlled\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>TLS certificates at the edge and common pitfalls\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"managed-dns\">Managed DNS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Route 53\u003c/strong> on AWS and \u003cstrong>Azure DNS\u003c/strong> (or Azure Traffic Manager for routing) host the authoritative records for your domain. You create a zone, then point the registrar&#39;s name servers at the zone&#39;s name servers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws route53 list-hosted-zones --query 'HostedZones[].[Name,Id]' --output text\naws route53 list-resource-record-sets --hosted-zone-id Z0123456789ABC --query 'ResourceRecordSets[?Type==`A`].[Name,AliasTarget.DNSName]' --output text\naz network dns zone list --query '[].name' -o tsv\naz network dns record-set a list -g rg-dns -z example.com -o table\ndig +short NS example.com\ndig +short shop.example.com\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws route53 list-hosted-zones --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;HostedZones[].[Name,Id]&#x27;\u003c/span> --output text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws route53 list-resource-record-sets --hosted-zone-id Z0123456789ABC --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ResourceRecordSets[?Type==`A`].[Name,AliasTarget.DNSName]&#x27;\u003c/span> --output text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az network dns zone list --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;[].name&#x27;\u003c/span> -o tsv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az network dns record-set a list -g rg-dns -z example.com -o table\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dig +short NS example.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dig +short shop.example.com\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Apex domains (\u003ccode>example.com\u003c/code>) cannot be a CNAME. Route 53 and Azure DNS offer \u003cstrong>alias records\u003c/strong> that behave like a CNAME at the apex and point to a load balancer, CDN distribution or storage endpoint, with no extra query cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"records-worth-knowing\">Records worth knowing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Type\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Use\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>A / AAAA\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Name to IPv4 / IPv6 address\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>CNAME\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Name to another name (not at the apex)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>ALIAS (cloud-specific)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Apex or name to a cloud resource\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>MX\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mail servers\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>TXT\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Verification, SPF, DKIM, DMARC\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>CAA\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Which certificate authorities may issue\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>TTL controls caching. Lower it to 60 seconds \u003cstrong>before\u003c/strong> a planned migration, so the change takes effect quickly, and raise it again afterwards.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"routing-policies\">Routing policies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Beyond a simple answer, DNS can steer traffic:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Weighted\u003c/strong>: send 5 percent of traffic to a new stack for a canary\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Latency-based\u003c/strong>: answer with the region closest to the user\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Failover\u003c/strong>: answer with a standby when a health check on the primary fails\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Geolocation\u003c/strong>: answer by the user&#39;s country, for compliance or content\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resource &quot;aws_route53_record&quot; &quot;shop_primary&quot; {\n  zone_id        = aws_route53_zone.main.zone_id\n  name           = &quot;shop.example.com&quot;\n  type           = &quot;A&quot;\n  set_identifier = &quot;eu-primary&quot;\n\n  failover_routing_policy {\n    type = &quot;PRIMARY&quot;\n  }\n\n  health_check_id = aws_route53_health_check.eu.id\n\n  alias {\n    name                   = aws_lb.eu.dns_name\n    zone_id                = aws_lb.eu.zone_id\n    evaluate_target_health = true\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_route53_record&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop_primary&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  zone_id\u003c/span>        = aws_route53_zone.main.zone_id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span>           = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop.example.com&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span>           = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;A&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  set_identifier\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-primary&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n  failover_routing_policy {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    type\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;PRIMARY&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">\n  health_check_id\u003c/span> = aws_route53_health_check.eu.id\n\n  alias {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    name\u003c/span>                   = aws_lb.eu.dns_name\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    zone_id\u003c/span>                = aws_lb.eu.zone_id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    evaluate_target_health\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Health-check-driven failover depends on honest health checks. A check that only tests the load balancer will keep sending users to a region whose application is broken.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cdn-cloudfront-and-azure-front-door\">CDN: CloudFront and Azure Front Door\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>content delivery network\u003c/strong> has many edge locations that cache responses near users and terminate TLS there. It reduces latency, offloads your origin and absorbs spikes and some attacks. Azure offers Front Door and Azure CDN; AWS offers CloudFront.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Caching is controlled by headers from the origin and by CDN rules:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -sI https://shop.example.com/static/app.js | grep -iE &quot;cache-control|etag|x-cache|age|via&quot;\ncurl -sI https://shop.example.com/api/orders | grep -iE &quot;cache-control|x-cache&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -sI https://shop.example.com/static/app.js | grep -iE \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cache-control|etag|x-cache|age|via&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">x-cache: Hit from cloudfront\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">age: 4821\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -sI https://shop.example.com/api/orders | grep -iE \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cache-control|x-cache&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cache-control: no-store\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">x-cache: Miss from cloudfront\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Cache static assets for a long time with \u003cstrong>versioned file names\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>app.3f9a1.js\u003c/code>), so a new release uses a new URL and nothing needs purging. Never cache personalised or authenticated responses unless the cache key includes what makes them different. Purging (invalidation) exists but is slow and sometimes billed, so design to avoid it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"certificates-and-the-edge\">Certificates and the edge\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use managed certificates (AWS Certificate Manager, Azure-managed certificates) that renew automatically. CloudFront requires the certificate in the \u003ccode>us-east-1\u003c/code> region. Set CAA records, enforce HTTPS with a redirect, enable HSTS once you are certain everything serves HTTPS, and keep the origin reachable only from the CDN using a shared secret header or private connectivity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-migration-that-took-a-day-to-complete\">Incident: the migration that took a day to complete\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team cuts \u003ccode>shop.example.com\u003c/code> over to a new load balancer by editing the record. Some customers reach the new stack immediately, others still hit the old one eight hours later, and the old stack has already been shut down.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The record had a TTL of 86400 seconds, so resolvers cached the old answer for up to a day.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dig +noall +answer shop.example.com\ndig +noall +answer shop.example.com @ns-123.awsdns-45.com\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dig +noall +answer shop.example.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop.example.com. 83100 IN A 198.51.100.10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dig +noall +answer shop.example.com @ns-123.awsdns-45.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop.example.com. 86400 IN A 203.0.113.50\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The authoritative server already returns the new address, while resolvers hold the old one. Restore the old backend while the cache expires. Next time, lower the TTL to 60 seconds a full TTL-period before the change, make the switch, verify with several resolvers, and only decommission the old stack after the original TTL has passed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Changing DNS with a long TTL and no plan to wait\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a CNAME at the zone apex\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caching personalised pages at the CDN\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failover that depends on a shallow health check\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting the origin accept traffic directly, bypassing the CDN and its protections\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"managed-dns","text":"Managed DNS"},{"id":"records-worth-knowing","text":"Records worth knowing"},{"id":"routing-policies","text":"Routing policies"},{"id":"cdn-cloudfront-and-azure-front-door","text":"CDN: CloudFront and Azure Front Door"},{"id":"certificates-and-the-edge","text":"Certificates and the edge"},{"id":"incident-the-migration-that-took-a-day-to-complete","text":"Incident: the migration that took a day to complete"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Amazon Route 53","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/Welcome.html"},{"t":"Azure DNS overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/dns/dns-overview"},{"t":"Amazon CloudFront","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/Introduction.html"}],"text":"Users reach your application through a name, and often through a content delivery network before they ever touch your servers. Getting DNS, caching and certificates right makes a site faster and far more resilient, while getting them wrong causes outages that are hard to see from inside the cloud. What you will learn Managed DNS: hosted zones, record types and alias records Routing policies for failover and latency What a CDN does and how caching is controlled TLS certificates at the edge and common pitfalls Managed DNS Route 53 on AWS and Azure DNS (or Azure Traffic Manager for routing) host the authoritative records for your domain. You create a zone, then point the registrar&#39;s name servers at the zone&#39;s name servers. $ aws route53 list-hosted-zones --query &#x27;HostedZones[].[Name,Id]&#x27; --output text $ aws route53 list-resource-record-sets --hosted-zone-id Z0123456789ABC --query &#x27;ResourceRecordSets[?Type==`A`].[Name,AliasTarget.DNSName]&#x27; --output text $ az network dns zone list --query &#x27;[].name&#x27; -o tsv $ az network dns record-set a list -g rg-dns -z example.com -o table $ dig +short NS example.com $ dig +short shop.example.com Apex domains ( example.com ) cannot be a CNAME. Route 53 and Azure DNS offer alias records that behave like a CNAME at the apex and point to a load balancer, CDN distribution or storage endpoint, with no extra query cost. Records worth knowing Type Use A / AAAA Name to IPv4 / IPv6 address CNAME Name to another name (not at the apex) ALIAS (cloud-specific) Apex or name to a cloud resource MX Mail servers TXT Verification, SPF, DKIM, DMARC CAA Which certificate authorities may issue TTL controls caching. Lower it to 60 seconds before a planned migration, so the change takes effect quickly, and raise it again afterwards. Routing policies Beyond a simple answer, DNS can steer traffic: Weighted : send 5 percent of traffic to a new stack for a canary Latency-based : answer with the region closest to the user Failover : answer with a standby when a health check on the primary fails Geolocation : answer by the user&#39;s country, for compliance or content resource \"aws_route53_record\" \"shop_primary\" { zone_id = aws_route53_zone.main.zone_id name = \"shop.example.com\" type = \"A\" set_identifier = \"eu-primary\" failover_routing_policy { type = \"PRIMARY\" } health_check_id = aws_route53_health_check.eu.id alias { name = aws_lb.eu.dns_name zone_id = aws_lb.eu.zone_id evaluate_target_health = true } } Health-check-driven failover depends on honest health checks. A check that only tests the load balancer will keep sending users to a region whose application is broken. CDN: CloudFront and Azure Front Door A content delivery network has many edge locations that cache responses near users and terminate TLS there. It reduces latency, offloads your origin and absorbs spikes and some attacks. Azure offers Front Door and Azure CDN; AWS offers CloudFront. Caching is controlled by headers from the origin and by CDN rules: $ curl -sI https://shop.example.com/static/app.js | grep -iE \"cache-control|etag|x-cache|age|via\" cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable x-cache: Hit from cloudfront age: 4821 $ curl -sI https://shop.example.com/api/orders | grep -iE \"cache-control|x-cache\" cache-control: no-store x-cache: Miss from cloudfront Cache static assets for a long time with versioned file names ( app.3f9a1.js ), so a new release uses a new URL and nothing needs purging. Never cache personalised or authenticated responses unless the cache key includes what makes them different. Purging (invalidation) exists but is slow and sometimes billed, so design to avoid it. Certificates and the edge Use managed certificates (AWS Certificate Manager, Azure-managed certificates) that renew automatically. CloudFront requires the certificate in the us-east-1 region. Set CAA records, enforce HTTPS with a redirect, enable HSTS once you are certain everything serves HTTPS, and keep the origin reachable only "},"cloud/serverless":{"html":"\u003cp>Serverless does not mean no servers. It means you hand the provider the job of provisioning, patching and scaling, and pay for execution rather than idle time. It fits spiky, event-driven work very well and some steady, latency-sensitive work very badly, so you need to know the trade-offs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The function model on AWS Lambda and Azure Functions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Triggers: HTTP, queues, storage events and schedules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cold starts, concurrency, timeouts and memory settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failure handling: retries, dead-letter queues and idempotency\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-model\">The model\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You upload a small unit of code with a handler. The platform runs it on demand in response to an \u003cstrong>event\u003c/strong>, scales the number of concurrent copies automatically, and bills by invocation count and duration multiplied by memory size.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import json\nimport os\nimport boto3\n\ns3 = boto3.client(&quot;s3&quot;)\nBUCKET = os.environ[&quot;REPORT_BUCKET&quot;]\n\ndef handler(event, context):\n    for record in event[&quot;Records&quot;]:\n        body = json.loads(record[&quot;body&quot;])\n        key = f&quot;reports/{body['order_id']}.json&quot;\n        s3.put_object(Bucket=BUCKET, Key=key, Body=json.dumps(body).encode())\n    return {&quot;processed&quot;: len(event[&quot;Records&quot;])}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> json\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> os\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> boto3\n\ns3 = boto3.client(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;s3&quot;\u003c/span>)\nBUCKET = os.environ[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;REPORT_BUCKET&quot;\u003c/span>]\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">handler\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">event, context\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> record \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> event[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Records&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n        body = json.loads(record[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;body&quot;\u003c/span>])\n        key = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;reports/\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{body[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;order_id&#x27;\u003c/span>]}\u003c/span>.json&quot;\u003c/span>\n        s3.put_object(Bucket=BUCKET, Key=key, Body=json.dumps(body).encode())\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;processed&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(event[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Records&quot;\u003c/span>])}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Initialise clients \u003cstrong>outside\u003c/strong> the handler, as above, so warm invocations reuse connections instead of rebuilding them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Concept\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>AWS\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Azure\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Function service\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Lambda\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure Functions\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>HTTP front door\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>API Gateway or function URL\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>HTTP trigger, API Management\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Queue trigger\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>SQS\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Storage Queue or Service Bus\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Event routing\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>EventBridge\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Event Grid\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Workflow\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Step Functions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Durable Functions, Logic Apps\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Identity\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Execution role\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Managed identity\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"triggers\">Triggers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>HTTP\u003c/strong>: a request through a gateway invokes the function synchronously\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Queue\u003c/strong>: messages are read in batches, with the platform scaling consumers with queue depth\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Storage events\u003c/strong>: an object upload invokes a function to create thumbnails or scan files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Schedule\u003c/strong>: a cron expression runs maintenance tasks\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws lambda create-function --function-name order-worker --runtime python3.12 --handler app.handler --zip-file fileb://function.zip --role arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/order-worker\naws lambda invoke --function-name order-worker --payload '{&quot;Records&quot;:[]}' --cli-binary-format raw-in-base64-out out.json\naws lambda get-function-configuration --function-name order-worker --query '[MemorySize,Timeout,Runtime]'\naws logs tail /aws/lambda/order-worker --since 15m\nfunc start\naz functionapp list -o table\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws lambda create-function --function-name order-worker --runtime python3.12 --handler app.handler --zip-file fileb://function.zip --role arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/order-worker\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws lambda invoke --function-name order-worker --payload \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;Records&quot;:[]}&#x27;\u003c/span> --cli-binary-format raw-in-base64-out out.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws lambda get-function-configuration --function-name order-worker --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;[MemorySize,Timeout,Runtime]&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws logs \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> /aws/lambda/order-worker --since 15m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>func start\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az functionapp list -o table\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cold-starts-concurrency-and-limits\">Cold starts, concurrency and limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>cold start\u003c/strong> happens when the platform must create a new execution environment: download code, start the runtime, run initialisation. It adds latency from tens of milliseconds to a few seconds, depending on language, package size and VPC attachment. Mitigations are smaller packages, lighter runtimes, provisioned concurrency (AWS) or the Premium plan (Azure), and keeping initialisation lean.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Limits matter. A function has a maximum duration (15 minutes on Lambda), a memory range, payload size limits and an \u003cstrong>account concurrency limit\u003c/strong> shared across all functions. A burst can exhaust it and starve unrelated functions, so set \u003cstrong>reserved concurrency\u003c/strong> on critical functions and a cap on noisy ones. Memory also sets CPU: giving a function more memory often makes it finish faster, sometimes at a lower total cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-handling\">Failure handling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Queue and event triggers retry on error, so your code runs \u003cstrong>at least once\u003c/strong>. It must be safe to run twice.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def handler(event, context):\n    for record in event[&quot;Records&quot;]:\n        body = json.loads(record[&quot;body&quot;])\n        if already_processed(body[&quot;order_id&quot;]):\n            continue\n        process(body)\n        mark_processed(body[&quot;order_id&quot;])\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">handler\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">event, context\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> record \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> event[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Records&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n        body = json.loads(record[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;body&quot;\u003c/span>])\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> already_processed(body[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_id&quot;\u003c/span>]):\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">continue\u003c/span>\n        process(body)\n        mark_processed(body[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_id&quot;\u003c/span>])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Configure a \u003cstrong>dead-letter queue\u003c/strong> so messages that fail repeatedly are kept for inspection rather than retried forever or dropped. Set the queue&#39;s visibility timeout longer than the function&#39;s timeout, or messages will be delivered again while the first attempt is still running.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"serverless-cost-thinking\">Serverless cost thinking\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cost scales with usage, which is wonderful at low volume and can become the expensive choice at constant high volume, where a container or VM may be cheaper. Watch for a recursive trigger (a function that writes to the bucket that triggers it), unbounded retries, and over-provisioned memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-function-that-triggered-itself\">Incident: the function that triggered itself\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A function resizes images uploaded to a bucket and writes the result back to the same bucket. Within minutes the invocation count explodes to millions, the bill alarm fires, and the account&#39;s concurrency limit throttles every other function, including checkout.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Writing to the trigger bucket created a loop. Break it quickly, then fix the design:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws lambda put-function-concurrency --function-name image-resizer --reserved-concurrent-executions 0\naws s3api get-bucket-notification-configuration --bucket acme-uploads\naws lambda delete-function-event-source-mapping --uuid 1234abcd\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws lambda put-function-concurrency --function-name image-resizer --reserved-concurrent-executions 0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-bucket-notification-configuration --bucket acme-uploads\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws lambda delete-function-event-source-mapping --uuid 1234abcd\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Setting reserved concurrency to zero stops execution immediately. Then write outputs to a \u003cstrong>different bucket or prefix\u003c/strong>, filter the trigger by prefix (\u003ccode>uploads/\u003c/code>), set budget alarms and concurrency caps on every function, and use a dead-letter queue.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Creating clients inside the handler on every call\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming exactly-once delivery\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No concurrency limits, letting one function starve others\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Triggering a function from its own output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing serverless for steady heavy load without comparing cost\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-model","text":"The model"},{"id":"triggers","text":"Triggers"},{"id":"cold-starts-concurrency-and-limits","text":"Cold starts, concurrency and limits"},{"id":"failure-handling","text":"Failure handling"},{"id":"serverless-cost-thinking","text":"Serverless cost thinking"},{"id":"incident-the-function-that-triggered-itself","text":"Incident: the function that triggered itself"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Lambda developer guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/welcome.html"},{"t":"Azure Functions overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview"},{"t":"AWS documentation","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/"}],"text":"Serverless does not mean no servers. It means you hand the provider the job of provisioning, patching and scaling, and pay for execution rather than idle time. It fits spiky, event-driven work very well and some steady, latency-sensitive work very badly, so you need to know the trade-offs. What you will learn The function model on AWS Lambda and Azure Functions Triggers: HTTP, queues, storage events and schedules Cold starts, concurrency, timeouts and memory settings Failure handling: retries, dead-letter queues and idempotency The model You upload a small unit of code with a handler. The platform runs it on demand in response to an event , scales the number of concurrent copies automatically, and bills by invocation count and duration multiplied by memory size. import json import os import boto3 s3 = boto3.client( \"s3\" ) BUCKET = os.environ[ \"REPORT_BUCKET\" ] def handler ( event, context ): for record in event[ \"Records\" ]: body = json.loads(record[ \"body\" ]) key = f\"reports/ {body[ &#x27;order_id&#x27; ]} .json\" s3.put_object(Bucket=BUCKET, Key=key, Body=json.dumps(body).encode()) return { \"processed\" : len (event[ \"Records\" ])} Initialise clients outside the handler, as above, so warm invocations reuse connections instead of rebuilding them. Concept AWS Azure Function service Lambda Azure Functions HTTP front door API Gateway or function URL HTTP trigger, API Management Queue trigger SQS Storage Queue or Service Bus Event routing EventBridge Event Grid Workflow Step Functions Durable Functions, Logic Apps Identity Execution role Managed identity Triggers HTTP : a request through a gateway invokes the function synchronously Queue : messages are read in batches, with the platform scaling consumers with queue depth Storage events : an object upload invokes a function to create thumbnails or scan files Schedule : a cron expression runs maintenance tasks $ aws lambda create-function --function-name order-worker --runtime python3.12 --handler app.handler --zip-file fileb://function.zip --role arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/order-worker $ aws lambda invoke --function-name order-worker --payload &#x27;{\"Records\":[]}&#x27; --cli-binary-format raw-in-base64-out out.json $ aws lambda get-function-configuration --function-name order-worker --query &#x27;[MemorySize,Timeout,Runtime]&#x27; $ aws logs tail /aws/lambda/order-worker --since 15m $ func start $ az functionapp list -o table Cold starts, concurrency and limits A cold start happens when the platform must create a new execution environment: download code, start the runtime, run initialisation. It adds latency from tens of milliseconds to a few seconds, depending on language, package size and VPC attachment. Mitigations are smaller packages, lighter runtimes, provisioned concurrency (AWS) or the Premium plan (Azure), and keeping initialisation lean. Limits matter. A function has a maximum duration (15 minutes on Lambda), a memory range, payload size limits and an account concurrency limit shared across all functions. A burst can exhaust it and starve unrelated functions, so set reserved concurrency on critical functions and a cap on noisy ones. Memory also sets CPU: giving a function more memory often makes it finish faster, sometimes at a lower total cost. Failure handling Queue and event triggers retry on error, so your code runs at least once . It must be safe to run twice. def handler ( event, context ): for record in event[ \"Records\" ]: body = json.loads(record[ \"body\" ]) if already_processed(body[ \"order_id\" ]): continue process(body) mark_processed(body[ \"order_id\" ]) Configure a dead-letter queue so messages that fail repeatedly are kept for inspection rather than retried forever or dropped. Set the queue&#39;s visibility timeout longer than the function&#39;s timeout, or messages will be delivered again while the first attempt is still running. Serverless cost thinking Cost scales with usage, which is wonderful at low volume and can become the expensive choice at constant"},"cloud/kubernetes-on-cloud":{"html":"\u003cp>Running your own control plane is real work. EKS and AKS take over the API server, etcd and scheduler, so you manage worker nodes and workloads. The skill is knowing where the provider&#39;s responsibility ends and what you must still configure well: identity, networking, upgrades and cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What the provider manages and what you manage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating a cluster and connecting \u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Workload identity: giving Pods cloud permissions without keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Load balancers, node pools and upgrades\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shared-responsibility\">Shared responsibility\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Area\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Provider\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>You\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Control plane availability and patching\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>etcd backups\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Worker node OS patching\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Optional (managed node groups, auto-upgrade)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Choose and enable\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Kubernetes version upgrades\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Provides versions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Plan and run them\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Pod security, RBAC, network policy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Application deployment and scaling\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Managed does not mean hands-off. Versions go out of support, and an unsupported control plane is eventually upgraded for you, on the provider&#39;s schedule.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"creating-and-connecting\">Creating and connecting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"eksctl create cluster --name shop-prod --region eu-west-1 --version 1.31 --nodegroup-name general --node-type m6i.large --nodes 3 --nodes-min 3 --nodes-max 8 --managed\naws eks update-kubeconfig --name shop-prod --region eu-west-1\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\naz aks create --resource-group rg-shop --name shop-prod --node-count 3 --enable-managed-identity --enable-oidc-issuer --enable-workload-identity --network-plugin azure\naz aks get-credentials --resource-group rg-shop --name shop-prod\nkubectl get nodes\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>eksctl create cluster --name shop-prod --region eu-west-1 --version 1.31 --nodegroup-name general --node-type m6i.large --nodes 3 --nodes-min 3 --nodes-max 8 --managed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws eks update-kubeconfig --name shop-prod --region eu-west-1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes -o wide\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az aks create --resource-group rg-shop --name shop-prod --node-count 3 --enable-managed-identity --enable-oidc-issuer --enable-workload-identity --network-plugin azure\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az aks get-credentials --resource-group rg-shop --name shop-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For production, define clusters in Terraform rather than one-off commands, so they are reviewed, repeatable and reproducible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"workload-identity\">Workload identity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Pod that needs S3, a queue or a key vault should not carry access keys. Both clouds can map a Kubernetes \u003cstrong>service account\u003c/strong> to a cloud identity, so the Pod receives short-lived credentials automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>On EKS this is \u003cstrong>IAM Roles for Service Accounts\u003c/strong> (or EKS Pod Identity); on AKS it is \u003cstrong>Workload Identity\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: ServiceAccount\nmetadata:\n  name: orders-worker\n  namespace: shop\n  annotations:\n    eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/shop-orders-worker\n---\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: orders-worker\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  replicas: 2\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: orders-worker\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: orders-worker\n    spec:\n      serviceAccountName: orders-worker\n      containers:\n        - name: worker\n          image: registry.example.com/shop/orders-worker:2.1.0\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ServiceAccount\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orders-worker\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">annotations:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/shop-orders-worker\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">---\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apps/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Deployment\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orders-worker\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orders-worker\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orders-worker\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">serviceAccountName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orders-worker\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">worker\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/orders-worker:2.1.0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The IAM role&#39;s trust policy allows only that exact service account in that exact cluster. Scope each role to what one application needs. Granting node-level permissions instead gives every Pod on the node the same access, which is far too broad.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exposure-and-networking\">Exposure and networking\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Service of type \u003ccode>LoadBalancer\u003c/code> makes the cloud create a load balancer. An Ingress controller (the AWS Load Balancer Controller, or the Application Gateway or NGINX ingress on Azure) shares one load balancer across many hosts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get svc -n ingress-nginx\nkubectl get ingress -A\nkubectl describe svc shop-api -n shop | grep -iE &quot;loadbalancer|events&quot; -A3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get svc -n ingress-nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get ingress -A\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe svc shop-api -n shop | grep -iE \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;loadbalancer|events&quot;\u003c/span> -A3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With cloud-native CNIs (AWS VPC CNI, Azure CNI) every Pod uses a real address from the subnet, so a large cluster can exhaust a small subnet. Plan CIDR ranges before creation. Use private clusters or restrict the API endpoint to trusted networks, and use security groups or NSGs and network policies for traffic control.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"nodes-and-upgrades\">Nodes and upgrades\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>managed node groups\u003c/strong> (EKS) or \u003cstrong>node pools\u003c/strong> (AKS) with several instance types and zones. Keep system and user workloads in separate pools, use spot or low-priority capacity only for interruption-tolerant work, and attach a cluster autoscaler or Karpenter.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"eksctl upgrade cluster --name shop-prod --version 1.31 --approve\neksctl upgrade nodegroup --name general --cluster shop-prod --kubernetes-version 1.31\naz aks get-upgrades --resource-group rg-shop --name shop-prod -o table\naz aks upgrade --resource-group rg-shop --name shop-prod --kubernetes-version 1.31.2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>eksctl upgrade cluster --name shop-prod --version 1.31 --approve\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>eksctl upgrade nodegroup --name general --cluster shop-prod --kubernetes-version 1.31\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az aks get-upgrades --resource-group rg-shop --name shop-prod -o table\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az aks upgrade --resource-group rg-shop --name shop-prod --kubernetes-version 1.31.2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Upgrade one minor version at a time, staging first. Check deprecated API usage before upgrading.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-pod-that-could-read-every-bucket\">Incident: the Pod that could read every bucket\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A security review finds that a small internal tool, running on the cluster, can list and read every S3 bucket in the account. It uses no access keys. A penetration tester shows that anyone who gets a shell in any Pod can read customer exports.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The Pods inherited the \u003cstrong>node instance profile\u003c/strong>, which had broad S3 access to support an old batch job.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec deploy/internal-tool -n tools -- aws sts get-caller-identity\nkubectl exec deploy/internal-tool -n tools -- env | grep -E &quot;AWS_ROLE_ARN|AWS_WEB_IDENTITY&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> deploy/internal-tool -n tools -- aws sts get-caller-identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> deploy/internal-tool -n tools -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span> | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;AWS_ROLE_ARN|AWS_WEB_IDENTITY&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>No web identity variables means the Pod falls back to the node&#39;s role. Fix it by giving each workload its own service account and role, reducing the node role to the minimum the kubelet needs, and blocking Pod access to the instance metadata service (set the hop limit to 1 and require IMDSv2). Verify with \u003ccode>sts get-caller-identity\u003c/code> from a test Pod.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting broad permissions on the node role\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing a small subnet and running out of Pod addresses\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Public API endpoint open to the world\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring Kubernetes version support deadlines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating the cluster as a pet created by hand instead of code\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"shared-responsibility","text":"Shared responsibility"},{"id":"creating-and-connecting","text":"Creating and connecting"},{"id":"workload-identity","text":"Workload identity"},{"id":"exposure-and-networking","text":"Exposure and networking"},{"id":"nodes-and-upgrades","text":"Nodes and upgrades"},{"id":"incident-the-pod-that-could-read-every-bucket","text":"Incident: the Pod that could read every bucket"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Amazon EKS user guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/what-is-eks.html"},{"t":"Azure Kubernetes Service","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/aks/what-is-aks"},{"t":"EKS: IAM roles for service accounts","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/iam-roles-for-service-accounts.html"}],"text":"Running your own control plane is real work. EKS and AKS take over the API server, etcd and scheduler, so you manage worker nodes and workloads. The skill is knowing where the provider&#39;s responsibility ends and what you must still configure well: identity, networking, upgrades and cost. What you will learn What the provider manages and what you manage Creating a cluster and connecting kubectl Workload identity: giving Pods cloud permissions without keys Load balancers, node pools and upgrades Shared responsibility Area Provider You Control plane availability and patching Yes etcd backups Yes Worker node OS patching Optional (managed node groups, auto-upgrade) Choose and enable Kubernetes version upgrades Provides versions Plan and run them Pod security, RBAC, network policy Yes Application deployment and scaling Yes Managed does not mean hands-off. Versions go out of support, and an unsupported control plane is eventually upgraded for you, on the provider&#39;s schedule. Creating and connecting $ eksctl create cluster --name shop-prod --region eu-west-1 --version 1.31 --nodegroup-name general --node-type m6i.large --nodes 3 --nodes-min 3 --nodes-max 8 --managed $ aws eks update-kubeconfig --name shop-prod --region eu-west-1 $ kubectl get nodes -o wide $ az aks create --resource-group rg-shop --name shop-prod --node-count 3 --enable-managed-identity --enable-oidc-issuer --enable-workload-identity --network-plugin azure $ az aks get-credentials --resource-group rg-shop --name shop-prod $ kubectl get nodes For production, define clusters in Terraform rather than one-off commands, so they are reviewed, repeatable and reproducible. Workload identity A Pod that needs S3, a queue or a key vault should not carry access keys. Both clouds can map a Kubernetes service account to a cloud identity, so the Pod receives short-lived credentials automatically. On EKS this is IAM Roles for Service Accounts (or EKS Pod Identity); on AKS it is Workload Identity . apiVersion: v1 kind: ServiceAccount metadata: name: orders-worker namespace: shop annotations: eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/shop-orders-worker --- apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: name: orders-worker namespace: shop spec: replicas: 2 selector: matchLabels: app: orders-worker template: metadata: labels: app: orders-worker spec: serviceAccountName: orders-worker containers: - name: worker image: registry.example.com/shop/orders-worker:2.1.0 The IAM role&#39;s trust policy allows only that exact service account in that exact cluster. Scope each role to what one application needs. Granting node-level permissions instead gives every Pod on the node the same access, which is far too broad. Exposure and networking A Service of type LoadBalancer makes the cloud create a load balancer. An Ingress controller (the AWS Load Balancer Controller, or the Application Gateway or NGINX ingress on Azure) shares one load balancer across many hosts. $ kubectl get svc -n ingress-nginx $ kubectl get ingress -A $ kubectl describe svc shop-api -n shop | grep -iE \"loadbalancer|events\" -A3 With cloud-native CNIs (AWS VPC CNI, Azure CNI) every Pod uses a real address from the subnet, so a large cluster can exhaust a small subnet. Plan CIDR ranges before creation. Use private clusters or restrict the API endpoint to trusted networks, and use security groups or NSGs and network policies for traffic control. Nodes and upgrades Use managed node groups (EKS) or node pools (AKS) with several instance types and zones. Keep system and user workloads in separate pools, use spot or low-priority capacity only for interruption-tolerant work, and attach a cluster autoscaler or Karpenter. $ eksctl upgrade cluster --name shop-prod --version 1.31 --approve $ eksctl upgrade nodegroup --name general --cluster shop-prod --kubernetes-version 1.31 $ az aks get-upgrades --resource-group rg-shop --name shop-prod -o table $ az aks upgrade --resource-group rg-shop --name shop-prod --kubernete"},"cloud/monitoring-and-logging":{"html":"\u003cp>Every cloud service emits telemetry, but nothing reads it for you. Production needs three things from the platform: \u003cstrong>metrics\u003c/strong> that show health and capacity, \u003cstrong>logs\u003c/strong> that explain behaviour, and \u003cstrong>audit trails\u003c/strong> that record who changed what. Knowing the native tools is the foundation even if you later add Prometheus or a commercial platform.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Metrics, logs and audit trails on each cloud\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Querying logs with CloudWatch Logs Insights and KQL\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating alarms that notify the right people\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retention, cost and cross-account collection\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"which-service-does-what\">Which service does what\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>AWS\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Azure\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Metrics and alarms\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CloudWatch Metrics, Alarms\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure Monitor Metrics, Alerts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Application and system logs\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CloudWatch Logs\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Log Analytics workspace\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Audit of API actions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CloudTrail\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Activity Log\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Tracing\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>X-Ray\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Application Insights\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Dashboards\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CloudWatch Dashboards\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Workbooks, dashboards\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Metrics are numbers over time with short retention at full resolution. Logs are text events and are far more expensive to store and search. Audit trails are your security record, and must be protected from tampering.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"metrics-and-alarms\">Metrics and alarms\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudwatch list-metrics --namespace AWS/ApplicationELB --metric-name TargetResponseTime | head -20\naws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace AWS/EC2 --metric-name CPUUtilization --dimensions Name=InstanceId,Value=i-0abc123 --start-time 2025-06-02T00:00:00Z --end-time 2025-06-02T06:00:00Z --period 300 --statistics Average\naws cloudwatch describe-alarms --state-value ALARM --query 'MetricAlarms[].AlarmName'\naz monitor metrics list --resource /subscriptions/0000/resourceGroups/rg-shop/providers/Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/web-1 --metric &quot;Percentage CPU&quot; --interval PT5M\naz monitor metrics alert list -g rg-shop -o table\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudwatch list-metrics --namespace AWS/ApplicationELB --metric-name TargetResponseTime | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace AWS/EC2 --metric-name CPUUtilization --dimensions Name=InstanceId,Value=i-0abc123 --start-time 2025-06-02T00:00:00Z --end-time 2025-06-02T06:00:00Z --period 300 --statistics Average\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudwatch describe-alarms --state-value ALARM --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;MetricAlarms[].AlarmName&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az monitor metrics list --resource /subscriptions/0000/resourceGroups/rg-shop/providers/Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/web-1 --metric \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Percentage CPU&quot;\u003c/span> --interval PT5M\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az monitor metrics alert list -g rg-shop -o table\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A good alarm reflects user impact or imminent failure, has a clear owner, and links to a runbook.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resource &quot;aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm&quot; &quot;alb_5xx&quot; {\n  alarm_name          = &quot;shop-alb-5xx-rate&quot;\n  namespace           = &quot;AWS/ApplicationELB&quot;\n  metric_name         = &quot;HTTPCode_Target_5XX_Count&quot;\n  statistic           = &quot;Sum&quot;\n  period              = 60\n  evaluation_periods  = 5\n  datapoints_to_alarm = 3\n  threshold           = 20\n  comparison_operator = &quot;GreaterThanThreshold&quot;\n  treat_missing_data  = &quot;notBreaching&quot;\n  alarm_actions       = [aws_sns_topic.oncall.arn]\n  ok_actions          = [aws_sns_topic.oncall.arn]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;alb_5xx&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  alarm_name\u003c/span>          = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-alb-5xx-rate&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  namespace\u003c/span>           = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;AWS/ApplicationELB&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  metric_name\u003c/span>         = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;HTTPCode_Target_5XX_Count&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  statistic\u003c/span>           = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Sum&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  period\u003c/span>              = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  evaluation_periods\u003c/span>  = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  datapoints_to_alarm\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  threshold\u003c/span>           = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  comparison_operator\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;GreaterThanThreshold&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  treat_missing_data\u003c/span>  = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;notBreaching&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  alarm_actions\u003c/span>       = [aws_sns_topic.oncall.arn]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  ok_actions\u003c/span>          = [aws_sns_topic.oncall.arn]\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>datapoints_to_alarm\u003c/code> of 3 out of 5 reduces noise from single spikes. Choose \u003ccode>treat_missing_data\u003c/code> deliberately: for a metric that should always report, missing data should alarm.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"querying-logs\">Querying logs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Install an agent or enable the platform integration to ship logs, use structured JSON where possible, and learn the query language.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"fields @timestamp, @message\n| filter @message like /ERROR/\n| stats count() as errors by bin(5m)\n| sort @timestamp desc\n| limit 50\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">fields @timestamp, @message\n| filter @message like /ERROR/\n| stats count() as errors by bin(5m)\n| sort @timestamp desc\n| limit 50\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>That is CloudWatch Logs Insights. The Azure equivalent is \u003cstrong>Kusto Query Language (KQL)\u003c/strong> in Log Analytics:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"AppTraces\n| where TimeGenerated &gt; ago(1h) and SeverityLevel &gt;= 3\n| summarize errors = count() by bin(TimeGenerated, 5m), AppRoleName\n| order by TimeGenerated desc\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">AppTraces\n| where TimeGenerated &gt; ago(1h) and SeverityLevel &gt;= 3\n| summarize errors = count() by bin(TimeGenerated, 5m), AppRoleName\n| order by TimeGenerated desc\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws logs start-query --log-group-name /shop/api --start-time 1748822400 --end-time 1748826000 --query-string 'fields @timestamp, @message | filter @message like /ERROR/ | limit 20'\naws logs tail /shop/api --since 30m --follow\naz monitor log-analytics query -w 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000 --analytics-query &quot;AppTraces | take 10&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws logs start-query --log-group-name /shop/api --start-time 1748822400 --end-time 1748826000 --query-string \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;fields @timestamp, @message | filter @message like /ERROR/ | limit 20&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws logs \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> /shop/api --since 30m --follow\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az monitor log-analytics query -w 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000 --analytics-query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;AppTraces | take 10&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"audit-trails\">Audit trails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>CloudTrail and the Azure Activity Log answer security and troubleshooting questions: who deleted this, when did the security group change, which role was assumed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=DeleteSecurityGroup --max-results 5\naz monitor activity-log list --offset 24h --query &quot;[?authorization.action=='Microsoft.Network/networkSecurityGroups/delete']&quot; -o table\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=DeleteSecurityGroup --max-results 5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az monitor activity-log list --offset 24h --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;[?authorization.action==&#x27;Microsoft.Network/networkSecurityGroups/delete&#x27;]&quot;\u003c/span> -o table\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Enable the trail in \u003cstrong>every region and account\u003c/strong>, send it to a separate, locked-down log archive account with object lock or immutability, and turn on integrity validation. An attacker&#39;s first step is often to disable or delete logging.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention-and-cost\">Retention and cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Log ingestion and retention are among the fastest-growing cloud bills.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Set a retention period on every log group, since the default is often &quot;never expire&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sample or drop noisy debug logs in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Archive old logs to cheap object storage, and query them with Athena or Azure Data Explorer when needed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alert on ingestion volume spikes, since a logging loop can cost thousands of dollars in a weekend\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-outage-nobody-was-paged-for\">Incident: the outage nobody was paged for\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Customers cannot check out for 40 minutes before a support ticket reveals it. All dashboards were green. The alarm on instance CPU never fired, and the only alert on the load balancer watched for zero healthy hosts.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The service was up but returning errors. Look at what the platform recorded:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace AWS/ApplicationELB --metric-name HTTPCode_Target_5XX_Count --dimensions Name=LoadBalancer,Value=app/shop/abc123 --start-time 2025-06-02T10:00:00Z --end-time 2025-06-02T11:00:00Z --period 60 --statistics Sum\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace AWS/ApplicationELB --metric-name HTTPCode_Target_5XX_Count --dimensions Name=LoadBalancer,Value=app/shop/abc123 --start-time 2025-06-02T10:00:00Z --end-time 2025-06-02T11:00:00Z --period 60 --statistics Sum\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The data shows hundreds of 5xx responses per minute starting at 10:12. Infrastructure alarms measured resources, not user experience. Add alarms on error rate and latency, an alarm on missing data, a synthetic checkout probe from outside, and route alerts to the on-call rotation with a runbook link.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Alarms only on CPU and disk, none on errors and latency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logs with unlimited retention and no budget alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Audit trail in the same account that attackers can modify\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unstructured logs that cannot be queried\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerts that go to an email nobody watches\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"which-service-does-what","text":"Which service does what"},{"id":"metrics-and-alarms","text":"Metrics and alarms"},{"id":"querying-logs","text":"Querying logs"},{"id":"audit-trails","text":"Audit trails"},{"id":"retention-and-cost","text":"Retention and cost"},{"id":"incident-the-outage-nobody-was-paged-for","text":"Incident: the outage nobody was paged for"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Amazon CloudWatch","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/WhatIsCloudWatch.html"},{"t":"Azure Monitor overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/overview"},{"t":"AWS CloudTrail","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html"}],"text":"Every cloud service emits telemetry, but nothing reads it for you. Production needs three things from the platform: metrics that show health and capacity, logs that explain behaviour, and audit trails that record who changed what. Knowing the native tools is the foundation even if you later add Prometheus or a commercial platform. What you will learn Metrics, logs and audit trails on each cloud Querying logs with CloudWatch Logs Insights and KQL Creating alarms that notify the right people Retention, cost and cross-account collection Which service does what Purpose AWS Azure Metrics and alarms CloudWatch Metrics, Alarms Azure Monitor Metrics, Alerts Application and system logs CloudWatch Logs Log Analytics workspace Audit of API actions CloudTrail Activity Log Tracing X-Ray Application Insights Dashboards CloudWatch Dashboards Workbooks, dashboards Metrics are numbers over time with short retention at full resolution. Logs are text events and are far more expensive to store and search. Audit trails are your security record, and must be protected from tampering. Metrics and alarms $ aws cloudwatch list-metrics --namespace AWS/ApplicationELB --metric-name TargetResponseTime | head -20 $ aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace AWS/EC2 --metric-name CPUUtilization --dimensions Name=InstanceId,Value=i-0abc123 --start-time 2025-06-02T00:00:00Z --end-time 2025-06-02T06:00:00Z --period 300 --statistics Average $ aws cloudwatch describe-alarms --state-value ALARM --query &#x27;MetricAlarms[].AlarmName&#x27; $ az monitor metrics list --resource /subscriptions/0000/resourceGroups/rg-shop/providers/Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/web-1 --metric \"Percentage CPU\" --interval PT5M $ az monitor metrics alert list -g rg-shop -o table A good alarm reflects user impact or imminent failure, has a clear owner, and links to a runbook. resource \"aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm\" \"alb_5xx\" { alarm_name = \"shop-alb-5xx-rate\" namespace = \"AWS/ApplicationELB\" metric_name = \"HTTPCode_Target_5XX_Count\" statistic = \"Sum\" period = 60 evaluation_periods = 5 datapoints_to_alarm = 3 threshold = 20 comparison_operator = \"GreaterThanThreshold\" treat_missing_data = \"notBreaching\" alarm_actions = [aws_sns_topic.oncall.arn] ok_actions = [aws_sns_topic.oncall.arn] } datapoints_to_alarm of 3 out of 5 reduces noise from single spikes. Choose treat_missing_data deliberately: for a metric that should always report, missing data should alarm. Querying logs Install an agent or enable the platform integration to ship logs, use structured JSON where possible, and learn the query language. fields @timestamp, @message | filter @message like /ERROR/ | stats count() as errors by bin(5m) | sort @timestamp desc | limit 50 That is CloudWatch Logs Insights. The Azure equivalent is Kusto Query Language (KQL) in Log Analytics: AppTraces | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) and SeverityLevel >= 3 | summarize errors = count() by bin(TimeGenerated, 5m), AppRoleName | order by TimeGenerated desc $ aws logs start-query --log-group-name /shop/api --start-time 1748822400 --end-time 1748826000 --query-string &#x27;fields @timestamp, @message | filter @message like /ERROR/ | limit 20&#x27; $ aws logs tail /shop/api --since 30m --follow $ az monitor log-analytics query -w 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000 --analytics-query \"AppTraces | take 10\" Audit trails CloudTrail and the Azure Activity Log answer security and troubleshooting questions: who deleted this, when did the security group change, which role was assumed. $ aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=DeleteSecurityGroup --max-results 5 $ az monitor activity-log list --offset 24h --query \"[?authorization.action==&#x27;Microsoft.Network/networkSecurityGroups/delete&#x27;]\" -o table Enable the trail in every region and account , send it to a separate, locked-down log archive account with object lock or immutability, and turn on integrity validation. An attacker&#39;s first step is often to"},"cloud/security-and-governance":{"html":"\u003cp>The cloud makes it easy to create resources and just as easy to create insecure ones. At scale you cannot review every change by hand. \u003cstrong>Governance\u003c/strong> means building guardrails so that safe is the default and unsafe is blocked or flagged automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Account and subscription structure for isolation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Preventive controls: SCPs, Azure Policy and permission boundaries\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Encryption, key management and secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Detective controls: posture management and findings\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"structure-for-isolation\">Structure for isolation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do not run everything in one account. Separate accounts (AWS) or subscriptions (Azure) give hard boundaries for blast radius, billing and access.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>AWS\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Azure\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Top\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Organization\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Tenant and management groups\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Unit of isolation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Account\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Subscription\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Grouping\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Organizational Units\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Management groups\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Guardrail\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Service control policies\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure Policy, RBAC deny assignments\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A common layout has separate accounts for production, non-production, shared networking, security tooling and the log archive. A compromise in a development account then cannot touch production data or erase audit logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preventive-guardrails\">Preventive guardrails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>service control policy\u003c/strong> sets the maximum permissions available in an account, whatever IAM says. Even an administrator inside the account cannot exceed it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n  &quot;Statement&quot;: [\n    {\n      &quot;Sid&quot;: &quot;DenyDisablingAudit&quot;,\n      &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Deny&quot;,\n      &quot;Action&quot;: [\n        &quot;cloudtrail:StopLogging&quot;,\n        &quot;cloudtrail:DeleteTrail&quot;,\n        &quot;config:StopConfigurationRecorder&quot;\n      ],\n      &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\n    },\n    {\n      &quot;Sid&quot;: &quot;DenyUnapprovedRegions&quot;,\n      &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Deny&quot;,\n      &quot;NotAction&quot;: [&quot;iam:*&quot;, &quot;organizations:*&quot;, &quot;route53:*&quot;, &quot;cloudfront:*&quot;, &quot;support:*&quot;],\n      &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;,\n      &quot;Condition&quot;: {\n        &quot;StringNotEquals&quot;: {\n          &quot;aws:RequestedRegion&quot;: [&quot;eu-west-1&quot;, &quot;eu-central-1&quot;]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Version&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2012-10-17&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Statement&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Sid&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DenyDisablingAudit&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Effect&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Deny&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Action&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cloudtrail:StopLogging&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cloudtrail:DeleteTrail&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;config:StopConfigurationRecorder&quot;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Resource&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Sid&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DenyUnapprovedRegions&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Effect&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Deny&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;NotAction&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;iam:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;organizations:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;route53:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cloudfront:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;support:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Resource&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Condition&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;StringNotEquals&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;aws:RequestedRegion&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-central-1&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>On Azure, \u003cstrong>Azure Policy\u003c/strong> can deny, audit or auto-remediate resources that break rules, such as storage accounts that allow public blob access or resources in the wrong region.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws organizations list-policies --filter SERVICE_CONTROL_POLICY\naws organizations list-accounts --query 'Accounts[].[Name,Id]' --output text\naz policy assignment list --query '[].{name:displayName,scope:scope}' -o table\naz policy state summarize --top 5\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws organizations list-policies --filter SERVICE_CONTROL_POLICY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws organizations list-accounts --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Accounts[].[Name,Id]&#x27;\u003c/span> --output text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az policy assignment list --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;[].{name:displayName,scope:scope}&#x27;\u003c/span> -o table\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az policy state summarize --top 5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Roll out guardrails in \u003cstrong>audit mode\u003c/strong> first, learn what would break, then enforce. A denied deployment with an unclear message creates support tickets, so write error text that explains the rule.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"data-protection\">Data protection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Encrypt data at rest with managed keys, and use customer-managed keys (KMS, Key Vault) where policy requires control and rotation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Require TLS for data in transit and block plain HTTP on storage endpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Block public access at the account level for object storage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep secrets in Secrets Manager or Key Vault, with access by identity and audit logging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separate key administrators from data users\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3control get-public-access-block --account-id 123456789012\naws kms list-aliases --query 'Aliases[?starts_with(AliasName, `alias/shop`)].AliasName'\naws kms get-key-rotation-status --key-id alias/shop-data\naz storage account show -n acmedata --query '{https:enableHttpsTrafficOnly, publicAccess:allowBlobPublicAccess, tls:minimumTlsVersion}'\naz keyvault show -n kv-shop --query '{purge:properties.enablePurgeProtection, soft:properties.enableSoftDelete}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3control get-public-access-block --account-id 123456789012\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws kms list-aliases --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Aliases[?starts_with(AliasName, `alias/shop`)].AliasName&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws kms get-key-rotation-status --key-id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">alias\u003c/span>/shop-data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az storage account show -n acmedata --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{https:enableHttpsTrafficOnly, publicAccess:allowBlobPublicAccess, tls:minimumTlsVersion}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az keyvault show -n kv-shop --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{purge:properties.enablePurgeProtection, soft:properties.enableSoftDelete}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"detective-controls\">Detective controls\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Prevention will not catch everything. Continuous assessment finds drift and misconfiguration: AWS Security Hub, Config and GuardDuty; Microsoft Defender for Cloud and Azure Policy compliance. Feed findings into a ticketing system with owners and deadlines, and prioritise by exposure: a public database matters more than a missing tag.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compliance-as-code\">Compliance as code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Express controls as code that runs in the pipeline before deployment, for example scanning Terraform with a policy engine, and run the same checks against live accounts. That keeps documentation, enforcement and evidence consistent for audits such as ISO 27001, SOC 2 or PCI DSS.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform show -json tfplan &gt; plan.json\ntrivy config --severity HIGH,CRITICAL .\ncheckov -d . --quiet --compact\naws securityhub get-findings --filters '{&quot;SeverityLabel&quot;:[{&quot;Value&quot;:&quot;CRITICAL&quot;,&quot;Comparison&quot;:&quot;EQUALS&quot;}],&quot;RecordState&quot;:[{&quot;Value&quot;:&quot;ACTIVE&quot;,&quot;Comparison&quot;:&quot;EQUALS&quot;}]}' --max-items 5\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform show -json tfplan &gt; plan.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy config --severity HIGH,CRITICAL .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>checkov -d . --quiet --compact\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws securityhub get-findings --filters \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;SeverityLabel&quot;:[{&quot;Value&quot;:&quot;CRITICAL&quot;,&quot;Comparison&quot;:&quot;EQUALS&quot;}],&quot;RecordState&quot;:[{&quot;Value&quot;:&quot;ACTIVE&quot;,&quot;Comparison&quot;:&quot;EQUALS&quot;}]}&#x27;\u003c/span> --max-items 5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-public-bucket-in-the-dev-account\">Incident: the public bucket in the dev account\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A researcher reports that a storage bucket named after your company lists customer invoices to anyone. A developer had created it in a sandbox account for a demo, copied real exports into it, and set a public-read policy to share a link.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Contain before you investigate:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3api put-public-access-block --bucket acme-demo-invoices --public-access-block-configuration BlockPublicAcls=true,IgnorePublicAcls=true,BlockPublicPolicy=true,RestrictPublicBuckets=true\naws s3api delete-bucket-policy --bucket acme-demo-invoices\naws s3api get-bucket-logging --bucket acme-demo-invoices\naws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=ResourceName,AttributeValue=acme-demo-invoices --max-results 20\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api put-public-access-block --bucket acme-demo-invoices --public-access-block-configuration BlockPublicAcls=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>,IgnorePublicAcls=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>,BlockPublicPolicy=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>,RestrictPublicBuckets=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api delete-bucket-policy --bucket acme-demo-invoices\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-bucket-logging --bucket acme-demo-invoices\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=ResourceName,AttributeValue=acme-demo-invoices --max-results 20\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Block access, preserve logs, determine what was downloaded and by whom, and follow your breach-notification process. Prevent a repeat with an account-level public access block enforced by an SCP, a rule that production data may not leave production accounts, and automated detection that alerts within minutes when a bucket becomes public.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One account for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guardrails enforced without an audit period or helpful error messages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relying on documentation instead of automated controls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Key administrators who can also read all the data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Findings dashboards that nobody owns\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"structure-for-isolation","text":"Structure for isolation"},{"id":"preventive-guardrails","text":"Preventive guardrails"},{"id":"data-protection","text":"Data protection"},{"id":"detective-controls","text":"Detective controls"},{"id":"compliance-as-code","text":"Compliance as code"},{"id":"incident-the-public-bucket-in-the-dev-account","text":"Incident: the public bucket in the dev account"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Organizations: service control policies","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_scps.html"},{"t":"Azure Policy overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/overview"},{"t":"AWS Key Management Service","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/overview.html"}],"text":"The cloud makes it easy to create resources and just as easy to create insecure ones. At scale you cannot review every change by hand. Governance means building guardrails so that safe is the default and unsafe is blocked or flagged automatically. What you will learn Account and subscription structure for isolation Preventive controls: SCPs, Azure Policy and permission boundaries Encryption, key management and secrets Detective controls: posture management and findings Structure for isolation Do not run everything in one account. Separate accounts (AWS) or subscriptions (Azure) give hard boundaries for blast radius, billing and access. Level AWS Azure Top Organization Tenant and management groups Unit of isolation Account Subscription Grouping Organizational Units Management groups Guardrail Service control policies Azure Policy, RBAC deny assignments A common layout has separate accounts for production, non-production, shared networking, security tooling and the log archive. A compromise in a development account then cannot touch production data or erase audit logs. Preventive guardrails A service control policy sets the maximum permissions available in an account, whatever IAM says. Even an administrator inside the account cannot exceed it. { \"Version\" : \"2012-10-17\" , \"Statement\" : [ { \"Sid\" : \"DenyDisablingAudit\" , \"Effect\" : \"Deny\" , \"Action\" : [ \"cloudtrail:StopLogging\" , \"cloudtrail:DeleteTrail\" , \"config:StopConfigurationRecorder\" ] , \"Resource\" : \"*\" } , { \"Sid\" : \"DenyUnapprovedRegions\" , \"Effect\" : \"Deny\" , \"NotAction\" : [ \"iam:*\" , \"organizations:*\" , \"route53:*\" , \"cloudfront:*\" , \"support:*\" ] , \"Resource\" : \"*\" , \"Condition\" : { \"StringNotEquals\" : { \"aws:RequestedRegion\" : [ \"eu-west-1\" , \"eu-central-1\" ] } } } ] } On Azure, Azure Policy can deny, audit or auto-remediate resources that break rules, such as storage accounts that allow public blob access or resources in the wrong region. $ aws organizations list-policies --filter SERVICE_CONTROL_POLICY $ aws organizations list-accounts --query &#x27;Accounts[].[Name,Id]&#x27; --output text $ az policy assignment list --query &#x27;[].{name:displayName,scope:scope}&#x27; -o table $ az policy state summarize --top 5 Roll out guardrails in audit mode first, learn what would break, then enforce. A denied deployment with an unclear message creates support tickets, so write error text that explains the rule. Data protection Encrypt data at rest with managed keys, and use customer-managed keys (KMS, Key Vault) where policy requires control and rotation Require TLS for data in transit and block plain HTTP on storage endpoints Block public access at the account level for object storage Keep secrets in Secrets Manager or Key Vault, with access by identity and audit logging Separate key administrators from data users $ aws s3control get-public-access-block --account-id 123456789012 $ aws kms list-aliases --query &#x27;Aliases[?starts_with(AliasName, `alias/shop`)].AliasName&#x27; $ aws kms get-key-rotation-status --key-id alias /shop-data $ az storage account show -n acmedata --query &#x27;{https:enableHttpsTrafficOnly, publicAccess:allowBlobPublicAccess, tls:minimumTlsVersion}&#x27; $ az keyvault show -n kv-shop --query &#x27;{purge:properties.enablePurgeProtection, soft:properties.enableSoftDelete}&#x27; Detective controls Prevention will not catch everything. Continuous assessment finds drift and misconfiguration: AWS Security Hub, Config and GuardDuty; Microsoft Defender for Cloud and Azure Policy compliance. Feed findings into a ticketing system with owners and deadlines, and prioritise by exposure: a public database matters more than a missing tag. Compliance as code Express controls as code that runs in the pipeline before deployment, for example scanning Terraform with a policy engine, and run the same checks against live accounts. That keeps documentation, enforcement and evidence consistent for audits such as ISO 27001, SOC 2 or PCI DSS. $ terraform show -json tfp"},"cloud/managed-services-and-cost":{"html":"\u003cp>Operating a database yourself means owning backups, patching, failover and tuning. A managed service takes most of that work, and you pay for it in money and some loss of control. Deciding what to run yourself and what to rent is one of the most valuable judgments a cloud engineer makes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Common managed services and what they replace\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>High availability versus disaster recovery\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Recovery objectives (RTO and RPO)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A cost-control routine\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"managed-services-worth-knowing\">Managed services worth knowing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Need\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>AWS\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Azure\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Relational database\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>RDS, Aurora\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure Database for PostgreSQL, MySQL; Azure SQL\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Key-value / document store\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>DynamoDB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Cosmos DB\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cache\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>ElastiCache\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure Cache for Redis\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Queue and messaging\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>SQS, SNS, EventBridge\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Service Bus, Event Grid\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>DNS\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Route 53\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure DNS\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Content delivery\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CloudFront\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure Front Door, CDN\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Secrets\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Secrets Manager, Parameter Store\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Key Vault\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Monitoring\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CloudWatch\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure Monitor\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Queues deserve a special mention. Putting a queue between components absorbs bursts, lets a slow consumer catch up, and means a failure in one part does not instantly break the rest. Design consumers to be \u003cstrong>idempotent\u003c/strong>, because delivery may be repeated.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"high-availability-and-disaster-recovery\">High availability and disaster recovery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>They answer different questions:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>High availability (HA)\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Disaster recovery (DR)\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Protects against\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A component or zone failing\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Losing a whole region or a data-destroying event\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Typical design\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Multiple zones, automatic failover\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Backups elsewhere, a second region\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Recovery\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Automatic, seconds to minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Planned, minutes to hours or more\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Two numbers define the target for any system:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>RTO\u003c/strong> (recovery time objective): how long can we be down?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>RPO\u003c/strong> (recovery point objective): how much recent data can we afford to lose?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>An RPO of 24 hours is met by nightly backups. An RPO of 5 minutes needs continuous replication. Tighter objectives cost much more, so agree on them with the business per system, not as one blanket number. A disaster recovery plan that has never been \u003cstrong>exercised\u003c/strong> is a hypothesis.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For a managed database, enable Multi-AZ (AWS) or zone-redundant HA (Azure) for availability, automated backups with point-in-time recovery for data protection, and deletion protection to stop an accidental removal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"infrastructure-that-fails-gracefully\">Infrastructure that fails gracefully\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Timeouts and retries with backoff\u003c/strong> on every network call\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Health checks\u003c/strong> that remove unhealthy instances\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Graceful degradation\u003c/strong>: if recommendations are down, still show the product page\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Limit blast radius\u003c/strong> with separate accounts, zones and per-service quotas\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Test failure\u003c/strong> deliberately: stop an instance, fail over a database, and see what actually happens\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"controlling-cost\">Controlling cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Practice\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Budgets with alerts at 50%, 80%, 100%\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No surprises at month end\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Tags: \u003ccode>owner\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>env\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>service\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Costs can be attributed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Review the top five cost items monthly\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Most savings sit in a few services\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Right-size from measured use\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stops paying for idle capacity\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Commitments for steady load\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Large discounts for predictable baseline\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Shut down non-production out of hours\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Often saves more than half of those costs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Delete orphans: disks, snapshots, IPs, load balancers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Removes waste with no impact\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws ec2 describe-volumes --filters Name=status,Values=available \\\n    --query 'Volumes[].{id:VolumeId,gb:Size}' --output table\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ec2 describe-volumes --filters Name=status,Values=available \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Volumes[].{id:VolumeId,gb:Size}&#x27;\u003c/span> --output table\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Unattached (&quot;available&quot;) volumes are paying for storage nobody uses.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Watch data transfer. Traffic between zones, out to the internet and between regions is billed, and chatty cross-zone designs can create surprising charges.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-database-failover-causes-an-outage-anyway\">Incident: a database failover causes an outage anyway\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The primary database&#39;s zone fails. Multi-AZ failover happens in about a minute, but the application stays down for 25 minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The database did its job. The application did not:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs deploy/shop-api -n shop --tail 4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs deploy/shop-api -n shop --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR connection refused to 10.20.20.15:5432\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR connection refused to 10.20.20.15:5432\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR connection refused to 10.20.20.15:5432\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The application connected using the database&#39;s \u003cstrong>IP address\u003c/strong>, which it cached. After failover the endpoint&#39;s DNS name points at the new primary, but the app never re-resolved it. Fixes: connect using the \u003cstrong>DNS endpoint\u003c/strong> the service provides, never an IP; keep DNS caching time low in the client; make the client reconnect with backoff when connections drop; and run a \u003cstrong>failover exercise\u003c/strong> on a schedule so you discover this in a test, not at 3 a.m. Also record the real recovery time from the exercise, since it is the honest figure to compare with the agreed RTO.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating Multi-AZ as a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using IP addresses instead of service DNS names\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting RTO and RPO as numbers nobody has tested\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No cost alerts or ownership tags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running self-managed databases without anyone responsible for restores\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"managed-services-worth-knowing","text":"Managed services worth knowing"},{"id":"high-availability-and-disaster-recovery","text":"High availability and disaster recovery"},{"id":"infrastructure-that-fails-gracefully","text":"Infrastructure that fails gracefully"},{"id":"controlling-cost","text":"Controlling cost"},{"id":"incident-a-database-failover-causes-an-outage-anyway","text":"Incident: a database failover causes an outage anyway"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Cost Management","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/what-is-costmanagement.html"},{"t":"Microsoft Cost Management","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/"},{"t":"AWS RDS user guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Welcome.html"}],"text":"Operating a database yourself means owning backups, patching, failover and tuning. A managed service takes most of that work, and you pay for it in money and some loss of control. Deciding what to run yourself and what to rent is one of the most valuable judgments a cloud engineer makes. What you will learn Common managed services and what they replace High availability versus disaster recovery Recovery objectives (RTO and RPO) A cost-control routine Managed services worth knowing Need AWS Azure Relational database RDS, Aurora Azure Database for PostgreSQL, MySQL; Azure SQL Key-value / document store DynamoDB Cosmos DB Cache ElastiCache Azure Cache for Redis Queue and messaging SQS, SNS, EventBridge Service Bus, Event Grid DNS Route 53 Azure DNS Content delivery CloudFront Azure Front Door, CDN Secrets Secrets Manager, Parameter Store Key Vault Monitoring CloudWatch Azure Monitor Queues deserve a special mention. Putting a queue between components absorbs bursts, lets a slow consumer catch up, and means a failure in one part does not instantly break the rest. Design consumers to be idempotent , because delivery may be repeated. High availability and disaster recovery They answer different questions: High availability (HA) Disaster recovery (DR) Protects against A component or zone failing Losing a whole region or a data-destroying event Typical design Multiple zones, automatic failover Backups elsewhere, a second region Recovery Automatic, seconds to minutes Planned, minutes to hours or more Two numbers define the target for any system: RTO (recovery time objective): how long can we be down? RPO (recovery point objective): how much recent data can we afford to lose? An RPO of 24 hours is met by nightly backups. An RPO of 5 minutes needs continuous replication. Tighter objectives cost much more, so agree on them with the business per system, not as one blanket number. A disaster recovery plan that has never been exercised is a hypothesis. For a managed database, enable Multi-AZ (AWS) or zone-redundant HA (Azure) for availability, automated backups with point-in-time recovery for data protection, and deletion protection to stop an accidental removal. Infrastructure that fails gracefully Timeouts and retries with backoff on every network call Health checks that remove unhealthy instances Graceful degradation : if recommendations are down, still show the product page Limit blast radius with separate accounts, zones and per-service quotas Test failure deliberately: stop an instance, fail over a database, and see what actually happens Controlling cost Practice Effect Budgets with alerts at 50%, 80%, 100% No surprises at month end Tags: owner , env , service Costs can be attributed Review the top five cost items monthly Most savings sit in a few services Right-size from measured use Stops paying for idle capacity Commitments for steady load Large discounts for predictable baseline Shut down non-production out of hours Often saves more than half of those costs Delete orphans: disks, snapshots, IPs, load balancers Removes waste with no impact $ aws ec2 describe-volumes --filters Name=status,Values=available \\ --query &#x27;Volumes[].{id:VolumeId,gb:Size}&#x27; --output table Unattached (\"available\") volumes are paying for storage nobody uses. Watch data transfer. Traffic between zones, out to the internet and between regions is billed, and chatty cross-zone designs can create surprising charges. Incident: a database failover causes an outage anyway Incident The primary database&#39;s zone fails. Multi-AZ failover happens in about a minute, but the application stays down for 25 minutes. The database did its job. The application did not: $ kubectl logs deploy/shop-api -n shop -- tail 4 ERROR connection refused to 10.20.20.15:5432 ERROR connection refused to 10.20.20.15:5432 ERROR connection refused to 10.20.20.15:5432 The application connected using the database&#39;s IP address , which it cached. After failover the endpoint&#39;s DNS na"},"cloud/high-availability-and-disaster-recovery":{"html":"\u003cp>Everything fails eventually: disks, hosts, whole data centres, sometimes an entire region, and often people (a bad deploy, a wrong command). High availability keeps a service running through small failures automatically. Disaster recovery restores it after large ones. They have different costs and different tests, and you need to choose them on purpose, based on what an hour of downtime costs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Failure domains: instance, zone, region, account, provider\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Recovery time objective (RTO) and recovery point objective (RPO)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The four disaster recovery patterns\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Data replication and its trade-offs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing: restore drills and failover exercises\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-domains\">Failure domains\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Domain\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical failure\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Protection\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Instance\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hardware or process crash\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Auto scaling, health checks, stateless design\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Zone\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Power or network issue in one data centre\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Spread across at least two or three zones\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Region\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Large regional outage, bad regional dependency\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Second region\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Account\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Compromised or deleted account, billing lock\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Backups in a separate account\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Human\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Wrong delete, bad deploy, ransomware\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Backups, versioning, immutable copies, change control\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Multi-zone design covers most hardware and data-centre failures cheaply and should be the default. Multi-region design is much more expensive and complex; use it when the business requires it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rto-and-rpo\">RTO and RPO\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>RTO\u003c/strong> is how long you can be down. \u003cstrong>RPO\u003c/strong> is how much data you can lose, measured in time since the last good copy. An RPO of 15 minutes needs backups or replication at least that often. Ask the business what each costs per hour, then pick a design that matches, not the biggest one available.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"disaster-recovery-patterns\">Disaster recovery patterns\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Pattern\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What runs in the second region\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>RTO\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>RPO\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Cost\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Backup and restore\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Only backups copied over\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hours\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hours\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Lowest\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Pilot light\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Data replicated; minimal core services off or tiny\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Tens of minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Low\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Warm standby\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Full stack at reduced size\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Seconds to minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Active-active\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Full stack serving traffic in both\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Near zero\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Near zero\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Highest\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"data-is-the-hard-part\">Data is the hard part\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Stateless services are easy to rebuild. Data needs replication: synchronous replication has no loss but adds latency and can stop writes if the link breaks; asynchronous replication is faster but may lose the latest writes. Replication copies mistakes too: a deleted table is deleted everywhere, so keep point-in-time recovery and separate backups. Test restores, because an untested backup is a hope.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traffic-and-automation\">Traffic and automation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use health-checked DNS or a global load balancer to move traffic. Keep DNS TTLs low enough for your RTO. Define everything as code so the second region can be created by a pipeline, not by memory. Keep secrets, container images and configuration available in the second region, and check that quotas there are high enough.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prove-it\">Prove it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws rds restore-db-instance-to-point-in-time \\\n    --source-db-instance-identifier shop-prod \\\n    --target-db-instance-identifier shop-restore-test \\\n    --restore-time 2026-10-20T09:00:00Z\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws rds restore-db-instance-to-point-in-time \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --source-db-instance-identifier shop-prod \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --target-db-instance-identifier shop-restore-test \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --restore-time 2026-10-20T09:00:00Z\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run restore drills each quarter and time them. Run a failover exercise (a game day) that actually moves traffic, and record the measured RTO and RPO. Update the runbook with what you learn.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your database is replicated to a second region, and an engineer drops a table by mistake. Does the replica save you?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No, the drop is replicated too. You need point-in-time recovery or a separate backup taken before the mistake.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Replication protects against hardware and site failure, not against logical errors.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-failover-that-did-not-work\">Incident: the failover that did not work\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A region becomes unavailable and the team starts a failover that had never been tested. It takes nine hours instead of the planned one.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform plan -var region=eu-west-1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform plan -var region=eu-west-1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: creating RDS Cluster: DBSubnetGroupDoesNotExist\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: InstanceLimitExceeded: You have requested more instances than your quota\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Network pieces were missing in the second region, the instance quota there was too low, and the latest container image had not been replicated. After the event the team replicated images, raised quotas, kept the stack as code that applies cleanly in both regions, and ran a quarterly failover drill with measured results.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating replication as backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never testing a restore or failover\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing multi-region without a business reason\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting quotas, secrets and images in the second region\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"failure-domains","text":"Failure domains"},{"id":"rto-and-rpo","text":"RTO and RPO"},{"id":"disaster-recovery-patterns","text":"Disaster recovery patterns"},{"id":"data-is-the-hard-part","text":"Data is the hard part"},{"id":"traffic-and-automation","text":"Traffic and automation"},{"id":"prove-it","text":"Prove it"},{"id":"incident-the-failover-that-did-not-work","text":"Incident: the failover that did not work"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Well-Architected: Reliability pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reliability-pillar/welcome.html"},{"t":"AWS: Disaster recovery of workloads","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/disaster-recovery-workloads-on-aws/disaster-recovery-workloads-on-aws.html"}],"text":"Everything fails eventually: disks, hosts, whole data centres, sometimes an entire region, and often people (a bad deploy, a wrong command). High availability keeps a service running through small failures automatically. Disaster recovery restores it after large ones. They have different costs and different tests, and you need to choose them on purpose, based on what an hour of downtime costs. What you will learn Failure domains: instance, zone, region, account, provider Recovery time objective (RTO) and recovery point objective (RPO) The four disaster recovery patterns Data replication and its trade-offs Testing: restore drills and failover exercises Failure domains Domain Typical failure Protection Instance Hardware or process crash Auto scaling, health checks, stateless design Zone Power or network issue in one data centre Spread across at least two or three zones Region Large regional outage, bad regional dependency Second region Account Compromised or deleted account, billing lock Backups in a separate account Human Wrong delete, bad deploy, ransomware Backups, versioning, immutable copies, change control Multi-zone design covers most hardware and data-centre failures cheaply and should be the default. Multi-region design is much more expensive and complex; use it when the business requires it. RTO and RPO RTO is how long you can be down. RPO is how much data you can lose, measured in time since the last good copy. An RPO of 15 minutes needs backups or replication at least that often. Ask the business what each costs per hour, then pick a design that matches, not the biggest one available. Disaster recovery patterns Pattern What runs in the second region RTO RPO Cost Backup and restore Only backups copied over Hours Hours Lowest Pilot light Data replicated; minimal core services off or tiny Tens of minutes Minutes Low Warm standby Full stack at reduced size Minutes Seconds to minutes Medium Active-active Full stack serving traffic in both Near zero Near zero Highest Data is the hard part Stateless services are easy to rebuild. Data needs replication: synchronous replication has no loss but adds latency and can stop writes if the link breaks; asynchronous replication is faster but may lose the latest writes. Replication copies mistakes too: a deleted table is deleted everywhere, so keep point-in-time recovery and separate backups. Test restores, because an untested backup is a hope. Traffic and automation Use health-checked DNS or a global load balancer to move traffic. Keep DNS TTLs low enough for your RTO. Define everything as code so the second region can be created by a pipeline, not by memory. Keep secrets, container images and configuration available in the second region, and check that quotas there are high enough. Prove it $ aws rds restore-db-instance-to-point-in-time \\ --source-db-instance-identifier shop-prod \\ --target-db-instance-identifier shop-restore-test \\ --restore-time 2026-10-20T09:00:00Z Run restore drills each quarter and time them. Run a failover exercise (a game day) that actually moves traffic, and record the measured RTO and RPO. Update the runbook with what you learn. Predict first. Your database is replicated to a second region, and an engineer drops a table by mistake. Does the replica save you? Your guess Answer: No, the drop is replicated too. You need point-in-time recovery or a separate backup taken before the mistake. Replication protects against hardware and site failure, not against logical errors. Incident: the failover that did not work Incident A region becomes unavailable and the team starts a failover that had never been tested. It takes nine hours instead of the planned one. $ terraform plan -var region=eu-west-1 Error: creating RDS Cluster: DBSubnetGroupDoesNotExist Error: InstanceLimitExceeded: You have requested more instances than your quota Network pieces were missing in the second region, the instance quota there was too low, and the latest container image had not been replic"},"cloud/landing-zones-and-multi-account-strategy":{"html":"\u003cp>A single cloud account for everything works at first. Then a test script deletes a production resource, a bill cannot be split between teams, a compromised key reaches everything, and a service limit used by one team blocks another. The answer is not more rules, but more boundaries: separate accounts (AWS), subscriptions (Azure) or projects (Google Cloud), organised under one management structure with shared guardrails.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why accounts are the strongest boundary\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A typical hierarchy and account roles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guardrails: organisation policies and service control policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Central logging, networking and identity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Vending new accounts with automation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-many-accounts\">Why many accounts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An account is a boundary for permissions, limits, billing and blast radius. Splitting by environment and workload gives you:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Security isolation:\u003c/strong> a compromise in development cannot reach production data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cost clarity:\u003c/strong> each account has its own bill and budget.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Independent limits:\u003c/strong> one team&#39;s quota use does not block another.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Simpler permissions:\u003c/strong> production access can be narrow, development access broad.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-typical-structure\">A typical structure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"Organization root\n  Security OU\n    log-archive          (central, write-only logs)\n    security-tooling     (detection, audit read access)\n  Infrastructure OU\n    network-hub          (shared networking, DNS, egress)\n    shared-services      (CI runners, registries)\n  Workloads OU\n    Prod OU:     app-a-prod, app-b-prod\n    Non-prod OU: app-a-dev,  app-a-staging\n  Sandbox OU             (experiments, tight budgets, auto-clean)\n  Management account     (billing and organisation only; no workloads)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">Organization root\n  Security OU\n    log-archive          (central, write-only logs)\n    security-tooling     (detection, audit read access)\n  Infrastructure OU\n    network-hub          (shared networking, DNS, egress)\n    shared-services      (CI runners, registries)\n  Workloads OU\n    Prod OU:     app-a-prod, app-b-prod\n    Non-prod OU: app-a-dev,  app-a-staging\n  Sandbox OU             (experiments, tight budgets, auto-clean)\n  Management account     (billing and organisation only; no workloads)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Keep workloads out of the management account. Organise by what needs different rules, not by the company&#39;s org chart.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"guardrails\">Guardrails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Policies attached to organisational units limit what even administrators in an account can do:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n  &quot;Statement&quot;: [{\n    &quot;Sid&quot;: &quot;DenyLeavingApprovedRegions&quot;,\n    &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Deny&quot;,\n    &quot;NotAction&quot;: [&quot;iam:*&quot;, &quot;organizations:*&quot;, &quot;support:*&quot;, &quot;route53:*&quot;, &quot;cloudfront:*&quot;],\n    &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;,\n    &quot;Condition&quot;: { &quot;StringNotEquals&quot;: { &quot;aws:RequestedRegion&quot;: [&quot;eu-west-1&quot;, &quot;eu-central-1&quot;] } }\n  }]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Version&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2012-10-17&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Statement&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Sid&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DenyLeavingApprovedRegions&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Effect&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Deny&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;NotAction&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;iam:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;organizations:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;support:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;route53:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cloudfront:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Resource&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Condition&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;StringNotEquals&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;aws:RequestedRegion&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-central-1&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Typical guardrails: allowed regions, no disabling of audit logging, no deleting central log buckets, no public storage, required encryption, no leaving the organisation. Test guardrails in a sandbox OU first, because a wrong deny can stop deployments.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shared-capabilities\">Shared capabilities\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Logging:\u003c/strong> audit logs from every account flow to a central, locked log archive account that workload admins cannot change.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Networking:\u003c/strong> a hub with shared egress, DNS and connectivity to on-premises; spokes connect through it. Plan address ranges across all accounts so they do not overlap.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Identity:\u003c/strong> people sign in through a central identity provider with single sign-on and get temporary roles per account, with no long-lived users in each account.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Security tooling:\u003c/strong> detection services enabled in all accounts and sent to one place.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"account-vending\">Account vending\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>New accounts should come from a pipeline: a request creates the account in the right OU, applies baseline guardrails, enables logging, sets budgets and alerts, creates roles and registers it in the inventory. Teams get a safe account in minutes without a ticket queue. Manage the baseline as code, and also plan for closing accounts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A workload admin tries to disable audit logging in their account. The organisation guardrail denies it. Why can a guardrail do this even for an administrator?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Organisation-level policies apply above the account's own permissions, so they limit everything inside it, including administrators.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Guardrails are boundaries, not trust in individuals.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-account-one-stolen-key\">Incident: one account, one stolen key\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A developer&#39;s access key leaks and an attacker starts instances for mining in the single account used for everything, including production databases.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws ce get-cost-and-usage --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-10-08 --granularity DAILY --metrics UnblendedCost\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ce get-cost-and-usage --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-10-08 --granularity DAILY --metrics UnblendedCost\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-10-06   412 USD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-10-07  9120 USD\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Because everything shared one account, the key reached production, and the bill could not be traced to a team. The company moved to an organisation with separate accounts for production and development, central logging in a locked account, region guardrails, short-lived single sign-on roles in place of keys, and per-account budget alerts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One account for all environments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running workloads in the management account\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overlapping network ranges across accounts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guardrails changed without testing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-many-accounts","text":"Why many accounts"},{"id":"a-typical-structure","text":"A typical structure"},{"id":"guardrails","text":"Guardrails"},{"id":"shared-capabilities","text":"Shared capabilities"},{"id":"account-vending","text":"Account vending"},{"id":"incident-one-account-one-stolen-key","text":"Incident: one account, one stolen key"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS: Organizing your AWS environment","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/organizing-your-aws-environment/organizing-your-aws-environment.html"},{"t":"Azure: Landing zones","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/landing-zone/"}],"text":"A single cloud account for everything works at first. Then a test script deletes a production resource, a bill cannot be split between teams, a compromised key reaches everything, and a service limit used by one team blocks another. The answer is not more rules, but more boundaries: separate accounts (AWS), subscriptions (Azure) or projects (Google Cloud), organised under one management structure with shared guardrails. What you will learn Why accounts are the strongest boundary A typical hierarchy and account roles Guardrails: organisation policies and service control policies Central logging, networking and identity Vending new accounts with automation Why many accounts An account is a boundary for permissions, limits, billing and blast radius. Splitting by environment and workload gives you: Security isolation: a compromise in development cannot reach production data. Cost clarity: each account has its own bill and budget. Independent limits: one team&#39;s quota use does not block another. Simpler permissions: production access can be narrow, development access broad. A typical structure Organization root Security OU log-archive (central, write-only logs) security-tooling (detection, audit read access) Infrastructure OU network-hub (shared networking, DNS, egress) shared-services (CI runners, registries) Workloads OU Prod OU: app-a-prod, app-b-prod Non-prod OU: app-a-dev, app-a-staging Sandbox OU (experiments, tight budgets, auto-clean) Management account (billing and organisation only; no workloads) Keep workloads out of the management account. Organise by what needs different rules, not by the company&#39;s org chart. Guardrails Policies attached to organisational units limit what even administrators in an account can do: { \"Version\" : \"2012-10-17\" , \"Statement\" : [ { \"Sid\" : \"DenyLeavingApprovedRegions\" , \"Effect\" : \"Deny\" , \"NotAction\" : [ \"iam:*\" , \"organizations:*\" , \"support:*\" , \"route53:*\" , \"cloudfront:*\" ] , \"Resource\" : \"*\" , \"Condition\" : { \"StringNotEquals\" : { \"aws:RequestedRegion\" : [ \"eu-west-1\" , \"eu-central-1\" ] } } } ] } Typical guardrails: allowed regions, no disabling of audit logging, no deleting central log buckets, no public storage, required encryption, no leaving the organisation. Test guardrails in a sandbox OU first, because a wrong deny can stop deployments. Shared capabilities Logging: audit logs from every account flow to a central, locked log archive account that workload admins cannot change. Networking: a hub with shared egress, DNS and connectivity to on-premises; spokes connect through it. Plan address ranges across all accounts so they do not overlap. Identity: people sign in through a central identity provider with single sign-on and get temporary roles per account, with no long-lived users in each account. Security tooling: detection services enabled in all accounts and sent to one place. Account vending New accounts should come from a pipeline: a request creates the account in the right OU, applies baseline guardrails, enables logging, sets budgets and alerts, creates roles and registers it in the inventory. Teams get a safe account in minutes without a ticket queue. Manage the baseline as code, and also plan for closing accounts. Predict first. A workload admin tries to disable audit logging in their account. The organisation guardrail denies it. Why can a guardrail do this even for an administrator? Your guess Answer: Organisation-level policies apply above the account's own permissions, so they limit everything inside it, including administrators. Guardrails are boundaries, not trust in individuals. Incident: one account, one stolen key Incident A developer&#39;s access key leaks and an attacker starts instances for mining in the single account used for everything, including production databases. $ aws ce get-cost-and-usage --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-10-08 --granularity DAILY --metrics UnblendedCost 2026-10-06 412 USD 2026-10-07 9120 USD Because everything shared one"},"cloud/object-storage-and-data-lifecycle":{"html":"\u003cp>Object storage looks like a bottomless folder, and that is why it causes two classic incidents: a bucket left public and a bill that quietly grows for years because nobody deleted old data. A handful of settings (access blocks, versioning, lifecycle rules and encryption) cover most of the risk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How object storage differs from a disk\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storage classes and lifecycle rules that control cost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Versioning, retention and protection from accidental deletion\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Blocking public access and using signed URLs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"objects-not-files\">Objects, not files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Object storage keeps whole objects (data plus metadata) in a flat namespace, addressed by key such as \u003ccode>reports/2026/01/sales.csv\u003c/code>. The slashes are only part of the name. You cannot edit a part of an object; you replace it. It is very durable (often described as eleven nines), scales without planning, and is accessed over HTTPS. It is not a file system, so it is a poor fit for databases or software that needs fast small writes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"classes-and-lifecycle-rules\">Classes and lifecycle rules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Providers offer classes that trade price for access speed: hot for frequent access, infrequent access for monthly reads, and archive for data you rarely need and can wait hours to get back. Lifecycle rules move or delete objects automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;Rules&quot;: [{\n    &quot;ID&quot;: &quot;logs-tiering&quot;,\n    &quot;Status&quot;: &quot;Enabled&quot;,\n    &quot;Filter&quot;: { &quot;Prefix&quot;: &quot;logs/&quot; },\n    &quot;Transitions&quot;: [\n      { &quot;Days&quot;: 30, &quot;StorageClass&quot;: &quot;STANDARD_IA&quot; },\n      { &quot;Days&quot;: 180, &quot;StorageClass&quot;: &quot;GLACIER&quot; }\n    ],\n    &quot;Expiration&quot;: { &quot;Days&quot;: 730 },\n    &quot;AbortIncompleteMultipartUpload&quot;: { &quot;DaysAfterInitiation&quot;: 7 }\n  }]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Rules&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;ID&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;logs-tiering&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Status&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Enabled&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Filter&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Prefix&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;logs/&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Transitions&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Days&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;StorageClass&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;STANDARD_IA&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Days&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">180\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;StorageClass&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;GLACIER&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Expiration&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Days&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">730\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;AbortIncompleteMultipartUpload&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;DaysAfterInitiation&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This rule moves logs to a cheaper class after 30 days, to archive after 180, deletes them after two years, and cleans up abandoned uploads. Check minimum storage durations and retrieval fees before you move small or short-lived objects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"versioning-and-deletion-protection\">Versioning and deletion protection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With \u003cstrong>versioning\u003c/strong>, an overwrite or delete creates a new version, and the old one stays recoverable. It protects against mistakes and some attacks, but old versions cost money, so add a lifecycle rule for noncurrent versions. For stronger protection use \u003cstrong>object lock\u003c/strong> (write once, read many) with a retention period, and require multi-factor authentication for delete. Replicate critical buckets to another region or account so a single account problem cannot remove every copy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-it-private\">Keep it private\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most exposures come from a permissive bucket policy or ACL. Turn on the account-wide \u003cstrong>block public access\u003c/strong> setting, and give applications access through roles, not shared keys. To share one file for a limited time, create a \u003cstrong>signed URL\u003c/strong> that expires:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3 presign s3://acme-reports/2026/01/sales.csv --expires-in 900\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3 presign s3://acme-reports/2026/01/sales.csv --expires-in 900\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Encrypt at rest with the provider key or your own key, require HTTPS in the bucket policy, and enable access logging on sensitive buckets. Run the provider&#39;s public-access checker regularly and alert when a bucket policy changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A bucket has versioning on and a user deletes an important object. Can you recover it, and what might still cost money later?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Yes, remove the delete marker or restore the previous version. Old versions keep costing money unless a lifecycle rule expires them.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Versioning protects data and creates cost you need to manage.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-bucket-that-kept-growing\">Incident: The bucket that kept growing\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A finance review found that a logging bucket cost more each month and no one could say why.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3 ls s3://acme-logs --recursive --summarize | tail -2\naws s3api get-bucket-lifecycle-configuration --bucket acme-logs\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> s3://acme-logs --recursive --summarize | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Total Objects: 94,220,118\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   Total Size: 412 TiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-bucket-lifecycle-configuration --bucket acme-logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">An error occurred (NoSuchLifecycleConfiguration)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Five years of logs sat in the most expensive class, with no expiry rule. Compliance needed only two years. A lifecycle rule moved data to infrequent access at 30 days and to archive at 180, and expired it after two years, which cut the bill by about 70 percent. The team also added a monthly report of the five largest buckets with their growth and owner tags.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No lifecycle rule, so data grows forever\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Public access allowed on a bucket by accident\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Versioning without expiring old versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating object storage like a fast local disk\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"objects-not-files","text":"Objects, not files"},{"id":"classes-and-lifecycle-rules","text":"Classes and lifecycle rules"},{"id":"versioning-and-deletion-protection","text":"Versioning and deletion protection"},{"id":"keep-it-private","text":"Keep it private"},{"id":"incident-the-bucket-that-kept-growing","text":"Incident: The bucket that kept growing"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Amazon S3 lifecycle","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html"},{"t":"Blocking public access to S3","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-control-block-public-access.html"}],"text":"Object storage looks like a bottomless folder, and that is why it causes two classic incidents: a bucket left public and a bill that quietly grows for years because nobody deleted old data. A handful of settings (access blocks, versioning, lifecycle rules and encryption) cover most of the risk. What you will learn How object storage differs from a disk Storage classes and lifecycle rules that control cost Versioning, retention and protection from accidental deletion Blocking public access and using signed URLs Objects, not files Object storage keeps whole objects (data plus metadata) in a flat namespace, addressed by key such as reports/2026/01/sales.csv . The slashes are only part of the name. You cannot edit a part of an object; you replace it. It is very durable (often described as eleven nines), scales without planning, and is accessed over HTTPS. It is not a file system, so it is a poor fit for databases or software that needs fast small writes. Classes and lifecycle rules Providers offer classes that trade price for access speed: hot for frequent access, infrequent access for monthly reads, and archive for data you rarely need and can wait hours to get back. Lifecycle rules move or delete objects automatically. { \"Rules\" : [ { \"ID\" : \"logs-tiering\" , \"Status\" : \"Enabled\" , \"Filter\" : { \"Prefix\" : \"logs/\" } , \"Transitions\" : [ { \"Days\" : 30 , \"StorageClass\" : \"STANDARD_IA\" } , { \"Days\" : 180 , \"StorageClass\" : \"GLACIER\" } ] , \"Expiration\" : { \"Days\" : 730 } , \"AbortIncompleteMultipartUpload\" : { \"DaysAfterInitiation\" : 7 } } ] } This rule moves logs to a cheaper class after 30 days, to archive after 180, deletes them after two years, and cleans up abandoned uploads. Check minimum storage durations and retrieval fees before you move small or short-lived objects. Versioning and deletion protection With versioning , an overwrite or delete creates a new version, and the old one stays recoverable. It protects against mistakes and some attacks, but old versions cost money, so add a lifecycle rule for noncurrent versions. For stronger protection use object lock (write once, read many) with a retention period, and require multi-factor authentication for delete. Replicate critical buckets to another region or account so a single account problem cannot remove every copy. Keep it private Most exposures come from a permissive bucket policy or ACL. Turn on the account-wide block public access setting, and give applications access through roles, not shared keys. To share one file for a limited time, create a signed URL that expires: $ aws s3 presign s3://acme-reports/2026/01/sales.csv --expires-in 900 Encrypt at rest with the provider key or your own key, require HTTPS in the bucket policy, and enable access logging on sensitive buckets. Run the provider&#39;s public-access checker regularly and alert when a bucket policy changes. Predict first. A bucket has versioning on and a user deletes an important object. Can you recover it, and what might still cost money later? Your guess Answer: Yes, remove the delete marker or restore the previous version. Old versions keep costing money unless a lifecycle rule expires them. Versioning protects data and creates cost you need to manage. Incident: The bucket that kept growing Incident A finance review found that a logging bucket cost more each month and no one could say why. $ aws s3 ls s3://acme-logs --recursive --summarize | tail -2 Total Objects: 94,220,118 Total Size: 412 TiB $ aws s3api get-bucket-lifecycle-configuration --bucket acme-logs An error occurred (NoSuchLifecycleConfiguration) Five years of logs sat in the most expensive class, with no expiry rule. Compliance needed only two years. A lifecycle rule moved data to infrequent access at 30 days and to archive at 180, and expired it after two years, which cut the bill by about 70 percent. The team also added a monthly report of the five largest buckets with their growth and owner tags. Common mistakes No lifecycle rule, so data grows"},"cloud/hybrid-connectivity-and-peering":{"html":"\u003cp>Two teams each built a network in the cloud, both using 10.0.0.0/16. When they try to connect them, nothing works, and changing an address range now means rebuilding everything. Connecting networks is mostly planning: addresses, routes and who may reach what.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Plan non-overlapping address ranges\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Peering, transit hubs, VPN and dedicated connections\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Private endpoints to reach cloud services without the internet\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Routes and security rules on both sides\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plan-addresses-first\">Plan addresses first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Networks can only be connected if their ranges do not overlap. Keep one address plan for the whole organization: a large block per cloud account or region, carved into subnets, with a spreadsheet or tool as the record. Reserve space for growth and for the office and data centre ranges. Overlap is the problem that is cheapest to avoid and most expensive to fix, because it can require rebuilding networks or using address translation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ways-to-connect\">Ways to connect\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Use it for\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Peering\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Two networks in the cloud\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Simple and fast, but not transitive (A to B and B to C does not give A to C)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Transit hub or gateway\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Many networks\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One central router with route tables; scales better than many peerings\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Site-to-site VPN\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Office or data centre to cloud\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Encrypted over the internet, quick to set up, speed depends on the internet\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Dedicated connection\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Heavy or steady traffic\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A private line from the provider: lower latency, higher cost, weeks to order\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Client VPN\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Individual engineers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Central access with logging and multi-factor authentication\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use a VPN as the backup for a dedicated line, so a cut fibre does not cut you off.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"private-endpoints\">Private endpoints\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cloud services such as storage or databases are usually reached over public addresses. A \u003cstrong>private endpoint\u003c/strong> (or interface endpoint) gives the service a private address inside your network, so traffic never leaves the provider network and you can block the internet path entirely. Combine it with a policy that says only your network may use the endpoint. Remember that private DNS must resolve the service name to the private address; otherwise clients still go to the public one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"routes-and-rules-on-both-sides\">Routes and rules on both sides\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A connection needs three things on each side: a route to the other range, a security rule that allows the traffic, and working DNS. Missing any one makes the link look broken.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ip route get 172.16.4.10\nping -c 2 172.16.4.10\nnc -zv -w3 172.16.4.10 5432\ndig +short db.corp.example\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ip route get 172.16.4.10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ping -c 2 172.16.4.10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>nc -zv -w3 172.16.4.10 5432\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dig +short db.corp.example\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Check in that order: route, reachability, port, name. Remember that peering and VPN connections need route table entries in every subnet that should use them, and that the other side must route replies back.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You peer network A with B, and B with C. Can A reach C?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No. Peering is not transitive. Use a transit hub, or peer A with C directly.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Each peering is a separate relationship between two networks.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-overlapping-ranges\">Incident: The overlapping ranges\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A migration project needed to connect a new analytics network to the production network, and the connection request was refused.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws ec2 describe-vpcs --query &quot;Vpcs[].[VpcId,CidrBlock]&quot; --output text\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ec2 describe-vpcs --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Vpcs[].[VpcId,CidrBlock]&quot;\u003c/span> --output text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vpc-0a1   10.0.0.0/16\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vpc-0b2   10.0.0.0/16\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Both networks used 10.0.0.0/16, and the provider cannot peer overlapping ranges. The analytics network was rebuilt in 10.40.0.0/16 over a weekend, and the organization introduced an address plan with a reviewed registry. New networks now get their range from the registry, and a pipeline check rejects any VPC definition whose range overlaps an existing one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Letting every team choose its own address range\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming peering is transitive\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding a route on one side only\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Connecting with VPN and forgetting that DNS must resolve names on both sides\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"plan-addresses-first","text":"Plan addresses first"},{"id":"ways-to-connect","text":"Ways to connect"},{"id":"private-endpoints","text":"Private endpoints"},{"id":"routes-and-rules-on-both-sides","text":"Routes and rules on both sides"},{"id":"incident-the-overlapping-ranges","text":"Incident: The overlapping ranges"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"VPC peering","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/peering/what-is-vpc-peering.html"},{"t":"AWS Site-to-Site VPN","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpn/latest/s2svpn/VPC_VPN.html"}],"text":"Two teams each built a network in the cloud, both using 10.0.0.0/16. When they try to connect them, nothing works, and changing an address range now means rebuilding everything. Connecting networks is mostly planning: addresses, routes and who may reach what. What you will learn Plan non-overlapping address ranges Peering, transit hubs, VPN and dedicated connections Private endpoints to reach cloud services without the internet Routes and security rules on both sides Plan addresses first Networks can only be connected if their ranges do not overlap. Keep one address plan for the whole organization: a large block per cloud account or region, carved into subnets, with a spreadsheet or tool as the record. Reserve space for growth and for the office and data centre ranges. Overlap is the problem that is cheapest to avoid and most expensive to fix, because it can require rebuilding networks or using address translation. Ways to connect Option Use it for Notes Peering Two networks in the cloud Simple and fast, but not transitive (A to B and B to C does not give A to C) Transit hub or gateway Many networks One central router with route tables; scales better than many peerings Site-to-site VPN Office or data centre to cloud Encrypted over the internet, quick to set up, speed depends on the internet Dedicated connection Heavy or steady traffic A private line from the provider: lower latency, higher cost, weeks to order Client VPN Individual engineers Central access with logging and multi-factor authentication Use a VPN as the backup for a dedicated line, so a cut fibre does not cut you off. Private endpoints Cloud services such as storage or databases are usually reached over public addresses. A private endpoint (or interface endpoint) gives the service a private address inside your network, so traffic never leaves the provider network and you can block the internet path entirely. Combine it with a policy that says only your network may use the endpoint. Remember that private DNS must resolve the service name to the private address; otherwise clients still go to the public one. Routes and rules on both sides A connection needs three things on each side: a route to the other range, a security rule that allows the traffic, and working DNS. Missing any one makes the link look broken. $ ip route get 172.16.4.10 $ ping -c 2 172.16.4.10 $ nc -zv -w3 172.16.4.10 5432 $ dig +short db.corp.example Check in that order: route, reachability, port, name. Remember that peering and VPN connections need route table entries in every subnet that should use them, and that the other side must route replies back. Predict first. You peer network A with B, and B with C. Can A reach C? Your guess Answer: No. Peering is not transitive. Use a transit hub, or peer A with C directly. Each peering is a separate relationship between two networks. Incident: The overlapping ranges Incident A migration project needed to connect a new analytics network to the production network, and the connection request was refused. $ aws ec2 describe-vpcs --query \"Vpcs[].[VpcId,CidrBlock]\" --output text vpc-0a1 10.0.0.0/16 vpc-0b2 10.0.0.0/16 Both networks used 10.0.0.0/16, and the provider cannot peer overlapping ranges. The analytics network was rebuilt in 10.40.0.0/16 over a weekend, and the organization introduced an address plan with a reviewed registry. New networks now get their range from the registry, and a pipeline check rejects any VPC definition whose range overlaps an existing one. Common mistakes Letting every team choose its own address range Assuming peering is transitive Adding a route on one side only Connecting with VPN and forgetting that DNS must resolve names on both sides"},"cloud/debugging-cloud-outages":{"html":"\u003cp>Cloud problems often look like application bugs. A request times out, an upload fails, or a deploy is denied. The causes are usually one of four things: an identity policy, a network rule, a quota or limit, or a service problem on the provider side. Each leaves a record if you know where to look.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading &quot;access denied&quot; correctly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Network path checks: security groups, route tables, network ACLs, flow logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Quotas and limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Provider status and who changed what\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"identity-who-was-denied-and-why\">Identity: who was denied, and why\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An AccessDenied error names the principal (the role or user), the action and the resource. Check them in order:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Is the caller the identity you think it is?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Does an identity policy allow the action on that resource?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Does a resource policy, permissions boundary, organisation policy or explicit deny override it?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws sts get-caller-identity                                     # which identity am I?\naws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn ROLE_ARN \\\n  --action-names s3:GetObject --resource-arns arn:aws:s3:::shop-data/*\naws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=GetObject --max-results 5\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">aws sts get-caller-identity                                     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># which identity am I?\u003c/span>\naws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn ROLE_ARN \\\n  --action-names s3:GetObject --resource-arns arn:aws:s3:::shop-data/*\naws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=GetObject --max-results 5\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Audit logs show the exact denied call with the error code and the calling role.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"network-follow-the-packet\">Network: follow the packet\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Check\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What to look for\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Security group\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Inbound and outbound rules on both ends; the source and port\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Route table\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A route to the destination (gateway, NAT, peering, endpoint)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Network ACL\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stateless rules: both directions must allow\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>DNS\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Private zone attached to the right network\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Flow logs\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>REJECT\u003c/code> records show who was blocked\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A private instance with no route to a NAT gateway cannot reach package repositories, and the error is a plain timeout.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"quotas-and-capacity\">Quotas and capacity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Limit exceeded&quot;, &quot;InsufficientInstanceCapacity&quot; and throttling errors are not bugs in your code. Check service quotas, request an increase early, and add retries with backoff and jitter. Capacity errors for one instance type in one zone can be avoided by allowing several types and zones.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"who-changed-what\">Who changed what\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=ResourceName,AttributeValue=sg-0abc --max-results 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=ResourceName,AttributeValue=sg-0abc --max-results 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">EventName: RevokeSecurityGroupIngress   Username: deploy-role   EventTime: 09:12\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A change record near the start of an outage is the strongest lead. Also check the provider&#39;s status page and your account&#39;s health notices, but confirm with your own metrics.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An instance in a private subnet cannot download updates; connections time out. Name two things to check.\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The route table for a route to a NAT gateway, and the security group and network ACL outbound rules.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A timeout means packets are dropped or have no path. Refusals and name errors point elsewhere.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"id&gt;audit;net&gt;audit;quota&gt;audit;svc&gt;audit\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Where a cloud request can be stopped\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"id\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"net\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Network path\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"quota\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Quota and capacity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"audit\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit and flow logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Identity to Audit and flow logs; 2, Network path to Audit and flow logs; 3, Quota and capacity to Audit and flow logs; 4, Service health to Audit and flow logs.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"id\">\u003cdt>Identity \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Credentials, role and policies decide whether the call is allowed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong role, explicit deny or boundary gives AccessDenied.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"net\">\u003cdt>Network path \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Security groups, routes, ACLs and endpoints decide whether packets arrive.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A missing route or rule shows as a timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"quota\">\u003cdt>Quota and capacity \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Service limits and regional capacity cap what you can create.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Limit errors look like random API failures.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Service health \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The provider service itself may be degraded.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Blaming your code while the provider has an incident.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"audit\">\u003cdt>Audit and flow logs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> CloudTrail-style logs and flow logs show what happened and who did it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Logging that was never enabled leaves no evidence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-deployment-that-lost-s3-access\">Incident: the deployment that lost S3 access\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>At 14:05 the nightly job begins failing with AccessDenied on a storage bucket it has used for a year.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=PutBucketPolicy --max-results 1\naws s3api get-bucket-policy --bucket shop-data | jq -r .Policy | grep -i -B2 -A4 Deny\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=PutBucketPolicy --max-results 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Username: platform-admin   EventTime: 14:02\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-bucket-policy --bucket shop-data | jq -r .Policy | grep -i -B2 -A4 Deny\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;Effect&quot;:&quot;Deny&quot;,&quot;Principal&quot;:&quot;*&quot;,&quot;Condition&quot;:{&quot;StringNotEquals&quot;:{&quot;aws:SourceVpce&quot;:&quot;vpce-0abc&quot;}}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Three minutes earlier a new bucket policy denied access except through one VPC endpoint. The job ran from another network, so it was blocked. The team added the second endpoint to the policy, and made bucket policy changes go through review with a simulation step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Granting broad permissions &quot;to test&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring explicit denies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No audit logging or flow logs when the incident happens\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying without backoff, which worsens throttling\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"identity-who-was-denied-and-why","text":"Identity: who was denied, and why"},{"id":"network-follow-the-packet","text":"Network: follow the packet"},{"id":"quotas-and-capacity","text":"Quotas and capacity"},{"id":"who-changed-what","text":"Who changed what"},{"id":"incident-the-deployment-that-lost-s3-access","text":"Incident: the deployment that lost S3 access"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS: Troubleshooting IAM","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/troubleshoot.html"},{"t":"AWS: VPC Flow Logs","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/flow-logs.html"}],"text":"Cloud problems often look like application bugs. A request times out, an upload fails, or a deploy is denied. The causes are usually one of four things: an identity policy, a network rule, a quota or limit, or a service problem on the provider side. Each leaves a record if you know where to look. What you will learn Reading \"access denied\" correctly Network path checks: security groups, route tables, network ACLs, flow logs Quotas and limits Provider status and who changed what Identity: who was denied, and why An AccessDenied error names the principal (the role or user), the action and the resource. Check them in order: Is the caller the identity you think it is? Does an identity policy allow the action on that resource? Does a resource policy, permissions boundary, organisation policy or explicit deny override it? aws sts get-caller-identity # which identity am I? aws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn ROLE_ARN \\ --action-names s3:GetObject --resource-arns arn:aws:s3:::shop-data/* aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=GetObject --max-results 5 Audit logs show the exact denied call with the error code and the calling role. Network: follow the packet Check What to look for Security group Inbound and outbound rules on both ends; the source and port Route table A route to the destination (gateway, NAT, peering, endpoint) Network ACL Stateless rules: both directions must allow DNS Private zone attached to the right network Flow logs REJECT records show who was blocked A private instance with no route to a NAT gateway cannot reach package repositories, and the error is a plain timeout. Quotas and capacity \"Limit exceeded\", \"InsufficientInstanceCapacity\" and throttling errors are not bugs in your code. Check service quotas, request an increase early, and add retries with backoff and jitter. Capacity errors for one instance type in one zone can be avoided by allowing several types and zones. Who changed what $ aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=ResourceName,AttributeValue=sg-0abc --max-results 3 EventName: RevokeSecurityGroupIngress Username: deploy-role EventTime: 09:12 A change record near the start of an outage is the strongest lead. Also check the provider&#39;s status page and your account&#39;s health notices, but confirm with your own metrics. Predict first. An instance in a private subnet cannot download updates; connections time out. Name two things to check. Your guess Answer: The route table for a route to a NAT gateway, and the security group and network ACL outbound rules. A timeout means packets are dropped or have no path. Refusals and name errors point elsewhere. Where a cloud request can be stopped Flow in order: 1, Identity to Audit and flow logs; 2, Network path to Audit and flow logs; 3, Quota and capacity to Audit and flow logs; 4, Service health to Audit and flow logs. Security Network edge Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Identity Security What it does: Credentials, role and policies decide whether the call is allowed. If it fails or is weak: Wrong role, explicit deny or boundary gives AccessDenied. Network path Network edge What it does: Security groups, routes, ACLs and endpoints decide whether packets arrive. If it fails or is weak: A missing route or rule shows as a timeout. Quota and capacity Step What it does: Service limits and regional capacity cap what you can create. If it fails or is weak: Limit errors look like random API failures. Service health Observability What it does: The provider service itself may be degraded. If it fails or is weak: Blaming your code while the provider has an incident. Audit and flow logs Observability What it does: CloudTrail-style logs and flow logs show what happened and who did it. If it fails or is weak: Logging that was never enabled leaves no evidence. Incident: the deployment t"}});